    text: "Full-directive review 2026-07-18 (post-PR#587 SDK relationship event platform + pm-vcs exemplar). Lane reconciled: pm-xtrd resolution-triple backfill, um4g/v1yo estimates + GH-585/586 pm-links, ju83 third-tranche evidence note, ds3b documentation-target expansion; pm-8ngt VCS-tranche acceptance recorded but story stays OPEN over active child pm-kr3t (lane decision honored). NEW items: pm-4e12 (public SDK atomic multi-item+relationship transaction boundary — the one GAP_REPORT.md waiver, under usfg) + five maintainer dogfood issues GH-588→pm-yp56 (comment no-text silent no-op), GH-589→pm-cj9v (assignee-perspective/claim conflation), GH-590→pm-i6pi (cycle-creating mutation silent deadlock, P1), GH-591→pm-6m1i (context agenda ~35% duplicate payloads), GH-592→pm-tmhs (pm-path-blind init recovery). Census 1868n/3105e/232a, zero actionable graph findings, degree≤1 = deliberate pair only. Nightly unchanged (pm-hvt3 both legs); release v2026.7.18 live; health ok:false = known pm-h90s disposition state."
  - created_at: "2026-07-19T03:45:24.438Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-19 full-directive review. Lanes PR#593 (7 agent-UX items closed zero-leak, GH-585/586/588..592), PR#594 (roadmap governance recorded: 73-command consolidation map, SDK-only sequence, million-item matrix, 22-part context catalog — new pm-szdc), PR#605 (commitWorkspaceTransaction shipped, pm-4e12 CLOSED, VCS GAP waiver retired; tranches on ju83/8ngt/ds3b) all reconciled clean — resolution 276 unchanged, estimates present, 8ngt correctly open over kr3t. Filed the maintainer dogfood merge-safety wave: 14 new items for GH-595/598..604/606..609/611/612 (family map + execution order = note on pm-g5sx), GH-610 mapped to pm-i6pi as duplicate, GH-596/597 pm-link comments backfilled. Census 1886n/3171e/242a fully accounted; zero actionable graph findings; sparse pair p5u6/x2vx unchanged. Nightly: same single pm-hvt3 spec on both legs, no drift. Health storage warn = known pm-h90s five actionable unknown-author events, unchanged. CLI-simplification / token-efficiency / long-term-planning directives verified already owned (n7rr map per PR#594, mpbb ledger, 4k6b catalog complete at 22, m2il scale matrix) — no new umbrellas, no duplicates."
  - created_at: "2026-07-19T04:08:25.382Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Full-directive review 2026-07-19 (second same-day pass; document-only): no new merges since the morning reconciliation - review focused on the PR#593/#594/#605 shipped surface plus repo-wide scans. Filed: pm-8xod (workspace-transaction journals outside gitignore block + no retention/GC; usfg follow-up of 4e12, g5sx family comment added), pm-853a (three divergent extension-flag value-arity predicates in cli/extension-command-help.ts; mpbb, ulqu class), pm-dvwm (about 4,100 template filler docstrings satisfying the 100 percent gates with zero information; n7rr). Evidence notes: yh8r census (main.ts AT 3400/3400 - next effective line fails CI; search 3371, loader 3367, extension 3349), 7ob5 (mutation-advisory double full-graph SCC per dep mutation - incremental-cache consumer), 9rxu (latency floor ~650-685ms unchanged, next improved to ~850ms). Clean sweeps: jscpd 0 clones at thresholds, docs-links/docs-skills green, deps minor-drift only (TS7 = do5b), in-repo packages lockstep 2026.7.18 zero-dep, MCP tool surface unchanged at 29/8744 tok, pm-f4yn dedupe honored (knip idea NOT re-filed), pm-cli-companion identified as maintainer private ops repo (no item). All validate warnings map to open owners; zero actionable."
  - created_at: "2026-07-19T11:22:39.579Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "Full-directive review 2026-07-19 (post-PR#614). Lane reconciliation: PR#614 shipped the ENTIRE merge-safety wave — wc1r/9q2t/m3nl/xdn6/cxyv/ol3p/gpo7/4uqm/8xod closed with triples+estimates; the only leak was GH-issue closure (GH-598/599/603/604/606/607/608/609/611 closed by me) and six unlinked new files (linked to their shipping items; orphaned_paths back to 0). Filed: pm-34yf (windows nightly POSIX assertion in vcs-extension.spec.ts from PR#605 — fixed spec-side same session, closed; windows leg now reduces to pm-hvt3), pm-iwsj (adopt merge drivers + CI storage-integrity/strict-exit gates in this repo — PR#614 migration steps not yet applied here), pm-i4fx (fence completeness class: relationships/*.jsonl uncovered + schema-added type folders drop coverage silently). Files baseline 172=121 moved+51 deleted on pm-j8z6. Resolution 276 unchanged; census 1891n/3194e, zero actionable graph findings.\n\nLong-horizon frontier after merge safety (sequencing, no new umbrellas): (1) Multi-agent durability endgame — iwsj adoption → i4fx fence drift → pibw cross-branch id collisions; with those, \"thousands of agents merging branches without tracker conflicts\" is provable via the PR#614 packed acceptance harness. (2) SDK-first endgame — ds3b reference docs → 9x6e zero import-boundary baseline; then usfg closes and the CLI is a pure SDK consumer. (3) Graph platform — ju83 (pm next's recommended) → 7ob5 incremental cached analytics (WorkspaceGraphCache consumer) → m2il million-item conformance matrix; 6x7o automates governance. (4) Context engine wave 3 — 801d impact-ordered substrate → 3hps; 4k6b catalog stays complete at 22, execution only. (5) Scale program 9rxu order unchanged: 801d → 0qg5 → lvyd → o79o. (6) Agent-surface simplification — n7rr 73-command consolidation map executes via 0z7n alias shim + e2bq tiered help; token diet continues via szdc/gdi7/nilh; dvwm docstring debt. (7) Distribution/positioning — human-gated decision queue unchanged (pbyu, vvib, agou, 7hye) → 69vv → lqu8; z3ez agent funnel. (8) Decade-scale — federation is1a and temporal digital-twin kr3t/8ngt remain the proven-primitives frontier now that cbwg/w89s universality closed and the 4e12 transaction waiver is retired."
  - created_at: "2026-07-19T14:43:57.065Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-19 (fourth same-day review, post-PR#616): SDK bulk-transaction wave SHIPPED — commitItemMutations atomic create/update/close batches (pm-y9hq), additive --add-ac/--remove-ac (pm-xh82), unmatched update-many id reporting (pm-ukml), empty-annotation guards across comments/notes/learnings via shared runAnnotationCommand (pm-d9g9/pm-iedg), packed-SDK @types/node correctness (pm-n1xx), and the pm-hvt3 staging canonicalization fix that should clear both red nightly legs (verification run 29691313876 dispatched). Lane was fully zero-leak on all four surfaces (item closes+triples, GH closures, pm-link comments, file links) — first lane to pass the post-PR#614 four-surface checklist unaided. Frontier movement: multi-agent durability endgame unchanged (iwsj -> i4fx -> pibw); NEW pm-xm7c extends the SDK-first lane with CLI/MCP exposure of the shipped transaction primitive (terminal+jq atomic batches for scripting agents), naming gated on pm-pbyu. Decision queue unchanged: pbyu/vvib/agou/7hye."
  - created_at: "2026-07-20T02:42:30.829Z"
    author: maintainer-agent
    text: "2026-07-20 review pass: PR#617 lane reconciled zero-leak (one unlinked spec backfilled to pm-x29o). MILESTONE — pm-g5sx merge-semantics program CLOSED: field-aware drivers for every artifact class, committed fence + drift audit, post-merge storage-integrity/history gates in CI, cross-branch id collision controls, MERGE_SAFETY.md + AGENT_GUIDE workflow docs, all SDK-first (PR#593/#594/#605/#614/#616/#617). Repository adopted ids.token_length 6 (pm-88cy; mixed 4/6-char ids from here on). Filed pm-x2aplf (config-set default materialization + stored-value flattening) and fixed pm-46octv docs syntax same pass. Planning census: all recurring-directive themes verified owned (n7rr/mpbb/4k6b/usfg/96h7/9rxu/dj98/o2kc + long-horizon m2il/xc68/is1a/kr3t/agou); frontier unchanged — pm next recommends pm-ju83."
  - created_at: "2026-07-21T15:56:00.750Z"
    author: codex-tracker-governance
    text: "Long-horizon organization 2026-07-21: near-term execution stays on the accepted command-grammar and SDK-first programs (pm-n7rr, pm-usfg, pm-9x6e, pm-ds3b) plus individually tracked security and quality findings. Mid-horizon work advances persistent reads, relevance signals and evaluation, immutable mutation-completeness, and graph reconstruction (pm-801d, pm-3hps, pm-atfm, pm-9yfo, pm-a8zm). Long-horizon work proves bounded-token million-item and multi-agent behavior, memory tiers, federation, and adaptive context utility (pm-9rxu, pm-5qmm, pm-is1a, pm-4k6b). Beyond-PM validation remains owned by pm-8ngt, pm-kr3t, pm-xc68, and the GA contract pm-agou. These are one connected roadmap, not parallel umbrellas."
  - created_at: "2026-07-22T18:26:35.403Z"
    author: codex-root
    text: "Living horizon map: Current foundations include pm-n7rr command simplification, pm-usfg SDK-boundary completion, pm-96h7 graph maturity, pm-4k6b adaptive context, pm-33cw governance, pm-o2kc durable history, pm-dj98 concurrency, and pm-u9d0/qwoy/92if quality gates. Emerging outcomes include pm-801d indexing, pm-atfm evaluation, pm-5qmm memory tiers, pm-g2zz million-item proof, pm-bwyw returning-agent continuity, and pm-m2il conformance. Expansion outcomes include pm-8ngt universal SDK proof, pm-is1a federation, pm-u14c signatures, pm-xc68 business policy, and pm-e200 event coordination. Exploratory outcomes include pm-kr3t temporal digital twins and only those pm-vvib mechanisms accepted through their decision gate. pm-m08hza defines promotion evidence across these horizons."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T06:22:45.857Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Post-ladder horizon themes, drafted 2026-07-25. The six outcome milestones carry the portfolio to 2028-06-30 and are gates, not workstreams; a seventh is never to be filed. This note records the themes that come after them, so that long-range direction exists as reviewable text and is promoted into epics under the existing milestones through the evidence-gated policy on pm-m08hza rather than by inventing new milestone rows.\n\nTheme A - the tracker becomes the substrate other tools are written against. The universal-domain milestone is proven today by two exemplars the project itself wrote. The horizon version is proven by systems the project did not write: third-party domains built on the SDK, an adapter contract that carries work in and out of external trackers (filed as pm-mn4zas), and a package ecosystem with enough trust signalling that a stranger's adapter is safe to install. The measurable form of this theme is the ratio of capability shipped as packages against capability shipped in core, and it should rise monotonically after GA.\n\nTheme B - context quality becomes a published, externally reproducible number. Today relevance is argued from design and measured internally. The horizon version publishes a benchmark with a methodology others can run, gates the project against it, and treats a regression in context quality as release-blocking in the same way a failing test is. Retrieval quality (filed as pm-p1g43q) and the context harness are the two halves; the public benchmark is already tracked and blocked on the harness.\n\nTheme C - provable history becomes independently verifiable by parties who do not trust the workspace owner. Hash chaining, per-entry author signatures, and a portable detached attestation are the three tracked components. The horizon version is a workspace whose entire recorded history can be handed to a third party who can verify, without the original tooling, both that nothing was altered and which principal recorded each entry. That is the point at which pm history is admissible evidence rather than internal bookkeeping, and it is the precondition for regulated and multi-organisation use.\n\nTheme D - fleets rather than agents. Every scale target currently expressed as thousands of concurrent agents is really about coordination quality, not throughput: safety is solved by atomic claim, but efficiency, capability matching, and critical-path staffing are not. Fleet allocation (filed as pm-3oqjnc) and fleet-shared context assembly (catalog entry 26 on pm-4k6b) are the first two components; the horizon adds cross-workspace federation so a fleet can span repositories.\n\nTheme E - the decade-shaped workspace. Memory tiers shipped, which makes a decade of closed work queryable in bounded tokens, but the surrounding policy has not been exercised: compaction cadence, archive boundaries, format migration across years of releases, and the question of what a workspace looks like when its oldest entries predate the current schema by several major shape changes. Cross-version compatibility and history growth policy are the tracked components; the horizon is a workspace that has genuinely run for years and can prove it.\n\nPromotion discipline: none of these themes becomes an item by being written here. Each becomes an epic under an existing capability or milestone only when its entry evidence exists, per the policy owned by pm-m08hza. This note is the source they are promoted from, and it should be extended rather than replaced."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T07:15:01.395Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Ecosystem review 2026-07-25 (CLI, SDK, packages, repo architecture, security, performance). Scope: live workspace state (1,984 items, 46 active, 234 open), full src/packages/scripts code review, published-artifact and dependency inspection, latency and token measurement on both a sandbox and the live workspace, agent-UX probes across command families, and the 18-repository external package fleet.\n\nHealth: pm health ok with settings, directories, storage integrity, history drift (1,984 checked, zero drifted) and vectorization all green; CI green on the last completed run of every workflow; jscpd clean at committed thresholds; zero TODO/FIXME/HACK in src; zero explicit any, zero ts-ignore.\n\nFive new items filed, each deduped against the full corpus before creation: pm-yse5dt (CLI transport overhead budget — the ~280ms per-invocation floor and the CLI-vs-SDK delta are unowned; cli.create 285ms vs sdk.create 9ms) · pm-998juj (published artifact weight — 40.66MB unpacked, 20MB of it inline-source sourcemaps already uploaded to Sentry separately) · pm-38bskj (public SDK surface shape — 881 exports behind one flat entry, 289ms eager import, no tiering, filed before pm-e6tm5c freezes it) · pm-185870 (subcommand-token error contract — four different error codes for one mistake class, no nearest-match recovery) · pm-ed28wi (linked-test commands are merge-unioned data executed with shell:true, no provenance or trust boundary).\n\nFindings routed to existing owners as notes rather than new items: skill duplication across three roots to pm-z3ez · output-cost baseline and the three-rung get depth ladder to pm-5t33or · the 78-command surface count to pm-wt43zj · 465 coverage-ignore pragmas concentrated in the largest files to pm-zclzll · root-level PRD.md and foreign toolchain caches to pm-kb5h · the full 146/146 suppression ledger to pm-j8vq · the pm validate 6s profile to pm-9rxu · single-item reads scaling with corpus size to pm-801d · the jscpd sensitivity gap quantified to pm-xspd · Record<string, unknown> residue on the public boundary to pm-usfg.\n\nNo gaps found in: relationship graph coverage, merge fence completeness (verified live that a custom schema type refreshes .gitattributes correctly), history integrity, changelog automation, validate warning ownership."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T18:01:39.376Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Directed review 2026-07-25 (CLI simplification, token efficiency, long-horizon planning, graph depth). Six items filed, all deduplicated against the 2,022-item corpus; the territory-level gap surface remains closed, so every one of the six came from live inspection of the shipped build rather than from re-reading the backlog.\n\nFiled:\n- pm-5gf0xf workspace-level point-in-time projection. The product documents this gap in its own source — get.ts refuses `--at` with `--tree` and with children projections because \"workspace-level historical projections are not yet indexed\" — and `pm list --at` is an unknown option. pm-hib1 shipped the single-item half; nothing owned the portfolio half.\n- pm-uey8od probabilistic delivery forecasting. Six deadlined outcome milestones and a promotion policy that demands evidence, with nothing computing whether the deadlines are achievable. pm-pee5 is the descriptive half (what happened); this is the predictive half, with a calibration ledger so the forecasts are scored rather than asserted.\n- pm-mav1ak uniform row-streaming contract. Measured: `--stream` exists on `list` alone, `--format ndjson` on list/search/context, and nothing on next/activity/graph/aggregate/deps/get — the surfaces with no answer are the ones that produce the largest row sets at scale.\n- pm-pww8ax capability dogfood coverage ratchet. This workspace exercises zero custom fields, zero custom statuses, zero applied profiles, zero Meeting and Event items. GH-450 (`--duration 5m` parses as months) survived precisely because nobody creates the type it breaks.\n- pm-08mt4k `pm next` recommends outcome-milestone gates as actionable work. Live: the single recommendation was pm-w7ccmv, a milestone with zero children, and pm-9rgaal appears in `ready[]`. The `decision_needed` bucket already proves the intended pattern for non-executable classes; milestones get no such treatment. Second half: `summary.containers: 27` names a bucket the envelope never emits.\n- pm-kpftft tracker data-quality ratchet in CI. No workflow runs `pm validate` or `pm health` on this repository's own tracker; the only pm self-check in CI is changelog drift. Two warning classes were traced to an item filed hours earlier by another harness without acceptance criteria or an estimate, caught by this review rather than by a gate.\n\nRepaired in place: pm-cragzs (GH-711) gained acceptance criteria, estimate, risk, confidence, and flag-parity lineage; the underlying asymmetry was verified live (`--allow-missing-parent` is present on `create --help`, absent on `update --help`).\n\nMeasurements recorded as notes on their owning items rather than as new items: surface census on pm-n7rr (77 commands, 1,712 flag declarations, 470 distinct spellings, worst offenders plan 97 / update 82 / create 78 / list 66); flag-axis analysis on pm-fs8q9x; output-cost table on pm-5t33or (TOON versus JSON is 13-37%, two-call cold start is ~15,230 bytes); intent-split evidence on pm-cxr0jb (`pm next` spends 47% of its payload on the blocked companion and 6% on the recommendation); mutation-op census on pm-9yfo (30 distinct ops across item streams, `_workspace.jsonl` holding 9 entries all of one op, confirming workspace-scope mutations sit entirely outside the append-only proof); files-baseline drift on pm-j8z6.\n\nContext algorithm catalog extended to (24) on pm-4k6b with owners recorded on pm-g8t7b9: (23) federated relevance calibration and cross-workspace budget allocation, closing the single-workspace assumption that every prior algorithm shares; (24) fleet-joint context diversification, closing the single-agent assumption. Both carry the catalog's standing degeneracy rule and reduce exactly to their predecessors at one source and one agent.\n\nGraph: 4,352 to 4,403 edges. Active-set semantic reachability from the six outcome milestones is complete (0 unreachable, max depth 6) and degree is saturated — the 28 remaining low-degree actives are all Semgrep findings whose hub topology is deliberate. The real deficit is terminal: 832 of 1,772 closed and canceled items carry zero semantic edges, 1,013 have no semantic path to an outcome, and 254 are unreachable even through the parent hierarchy. That baseline and a three-stage batch strategy are recorded on pm-lnrk, which owns it; it was deliberately not executed here, because pm-changelog buckets by `updated_at` and a sweep of 832 terminal items would falsify an entire release note."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T20:46:33.164Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Review pass 2026-07-25 (evening, post-PR#713) — reconciliation, live-build inspection, and typed-graph deepening.\n\nReconciliation. PR#713 (extensible agent identity and model provenance, merged 20:12Z) self-reconciled cleanly on all four surfaces: pm-qwuber, pm-03pq3o, pm-6uxhe0, pm-zqsrt5, pm-sx52hr, and pm-brxdct all closed with resolution and close_reason, source and doc links present, orphaned linked paths still zero, and every open GH issue (711, 681, 676, 675, 672, 651, 634) already mapped to exactly one item with its pm-link comment. Program state moved further than memory had it: pm-9x6e closed the SDK import boundary to zero private edges with a hard gate, pm-38bskj closed the surface tiering into eleven subpath entrypoints, and pm-e6tm5c closed the public-surface snapshot gate, so the SDK-first epic pm-usfg is now down to its own umbrella acceptance.\n\nThe abstract gap surface stayed exhausted, as it has since the pass that first declared it. Every new item this pass came from executing the shipped 2026.7.25 build and measuring it; none came from re-reading the backlog.\n\nFiled, five items, each deduped against the full corpus:\n- pm-rggtvd (P1, under pm-96h7) — hierarchy-canonicalizing dependency kinds bypass every cycle detector. Mutual child_of rows are accepted, validate lifecycle and pm graph analyze both report acyclic, and pm graph then names the same item as both ancestor and descendant. The parent scalar has had this protection since pm-8vul; the dependency channel never did.\n- pm-4020c5 (P2, under pm-96h7) — fifteen accepted dependency-kind spellings for roughly eight relations, canonicalized on write for exactly one alias and stored verbatim for the rest, so raw-store grep and jq consumers silently miss edges and the vocabulary keeps growing.\n- pm-9j2r3b (P1, under pm-o2kc, verifies pm-w7ccmv) — branch merge is an unrecorded mutation. The field-aware merge converges correctly but writes no history entry, so the merged state has no address, history --verify fails permanently, and history-repair correctly declines to fix it.\n- pm-z71aoy (P1, under pm-u9d0, verifies pm-j82fd3) — the token-budget gate's corpus is unrepresentative in scale (real next output is ten times its declared ceiling) and in coverage (the MCP handshake, the largest fixed per-session cost, grew 28 to 31 tools and 8.2k to 9.6k tokens with the gate green).\n- pm-nb4zwz (P3, under pm-mpbb) — read surfaces give no tombstone signal, so an agent cannot tell a never-existed id from a deleted and fully recoverable one.\n\nRouted as measured notes to existing owners rather than new items: unaudited-mutation inventory to pm-9yfo (focus and gc record nothing; the workspace stream carries two of the ten op kinds pm-klo8 scoped) · live merge conformance baseline to pm-76dnfg · diagnostic redundancy to pm-cha95z · resolution faucet at 287 with the eleven-item 07-21..23 window enumerated and deliberately not backfilled, to pm-wenq · read-path latency and paired token cost on the real tracker to pm-801d · restore, delete-tombstone, and version-identity findings to pm-wlqxg3.\n\nGraph. Typed semantic edges went from 40 to 80 (implements 15 to 41, verifies 25 to 37) plus two ordering edges, total 4,409 to 4,482, with no cycle introduced and lifecycle still ok. Every one of the six outcome milestones now carries at least two incoming verifies from active work, every active Story and open ADR has named implementers, and the active graph holds zero isolates and zero degree-one nodes with no unreachable active item. The deficit that remains is unchanged and still terminal-only, owned by pm-lnrk."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T21:19:39.109Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Ecosystem review 2026-07-25 (evening) — CLI, SDK, MCP, packages, repo structure. Method note for future passes: the read-only gap surface stays exhausted, and every item below came from EXECUTING the shipped build in a clean sandbox rather than from reading source. Four capabilities that the pm-pww8ax ratchet lists at zero workspace adoption (custom types, custom statuses, custom fields, extension-contributed surfaces) were exercised end-to-end for the first time, and three of the four leaked defects immediately.\n\nFILED (4):\n- pm-0mhspz (Issue P1, parent pm-8jdc) — a custom status registered without --role gets empty roles and no warning, and items in it become invisible to next/context/list-open while remaining non-terminal; validate and health both stay green. Control with --role active makes the identical item recommended. Second half: context summary.open/in_progress match the literal built-in status ids while active_items is role-derived, so the summary arithmetic stops reconciling in any customized workspace. Directly contradicts pm-8jdc's own acceptance criterion.\n- pm-c0wthb (Issue P1, parent pm-o2kc) — the merge fence fails OPEN. pm merge install writes bare `pm ...` driver commands; when pm is not on PATH the drivers die with \"command not found\", git marks the item .toon and history .jsonl as UU but writes ZERO conflict markers, and the worktree holds the ours side verbatim. Naive resolution (no markers, looks fine, git add) silently discards the other branch's fields AND its history entries. Control with pm on PATH produces a perfect field-aware union. Exposure: CI with a devDependency install, containers, npx, worktrees, GUI clients.\n- pm-m4ikkz (Feature P1, parent pm-mpbb) — the MCP tool surface is a static 31-tool snapshot blind to the runtime workspace. Custom types/statuses are usable but undeclared; extension commands run via pm_run but appear in no tool and in no action enumeration; custom fields are unreachable entirely, dropped at both top level and nested options while the warning claims they were \"passed through unchanged\" — the same warning text that accompanies title/type/status, which DO persist. The agent surface most agents actually drive is strictly less capable than the CLI.\n- pm-9smp7j (Issue P2, parent pm-ugqx) — `pm extension --init` and `pm package init` are divergent generators behind the same grammar. With identical arguments the extension path emits no test file, no .gitignore, and a package.json containing only {\"type\":\"module\"}; the package path emits a complete publishable manifest plus index.test.ts. Manifests are byte-identical apart from the name, and docs/EXTENSIONS.md documents only the good path.\n\nROUTED AS NOTES to existing owners (no new items, fences respected):\npm-801d point-read evidence — `pm get <id>` costs 1,053ms, MORE than `list --limit 10` at 821ms, on 2,033 items; ~750ms of pure corpus scan on every invocation; health 5,837ms alongside validate 7,192ms. pm-kb5h — three tracked benchmark roots, with scripts/benchmarks/ a one-file CI-unwired singleton colliding by pluralization with scripts/bench/. pm-f4yn — knip baseline 45 unused exports / 63 unused exported types / 3 duplicate exports, with the caveat that no knip config declares the SDK subpaths so the public merge primitives read as dead. pm-z3ez — the three skill roots have DIVERGED, not duplicated (pm-developer 74 vs 123 lines, pm-release 82 vs 19 lines, pm-audit vs pm-auditor). pm-quzx — eight external packages all on 2026.7.25 lockstep but with six different unbounded peer floors, two declaring no peer at all, and the scaffold minting the same pattern. pm-998juj — sourcemaps are now 28MB of the 40MB artifact, about 70%, up from roughly half; dead typebox peer still declared.\n\nVERIFIED HEALTHY, do not re-investigate: history drift 0 across 2,033 items; the field-aware merge itself produces correct unions (tags, priority and assignee from both branches, history unioned); custom fields round-trip perfectly through the CLI including search; schema add-type refreshes the .gitattributes merge fence; pm-g072's install-sources hardening is in place (both the `--` separator and the leading-dash rejection); only one `shell: true` in src, the known pm-ed28wi site; type safety strong at 2 `any` and 0 ts-ignore across src; scripts/prod telemetry correctly gitignored with zero tracked files; npm 2026.7.25 and all five CI workflows green."
  - created_at: "2026-07-25T21:42:45.158Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Ecosystem review 2026-07-25 (late evening). Scope: the shipped 2026.7.25 build end to end — CLI, SDK, MCP surface, first-party packages, profiles, merge path, repository organisation, duplication, type safety, dependency surface, and latency. Method as in the preceding reviews: every finding below came from executing the build in a clean sandbox or measuring the live tracker, not from re-reading the backlog. The gap surface has been exhausted since the 07-07 pass, so what is new is what only running the product reveals.\n\nFive items filed, each deduped against the full corpus and the never-refile ledger:\n\n- pm-z8qd4k (P1, under pm-dj98) — claim's documented atomic test-and-set has no unique operand in the configuration this project mandates. Automatic identity resolves every Claude Code process to the single author 'harness:claude-code', so a second agent's claim on a held item succeeds with exit 0 and no conflict; with two distinct PM_AUTHOR values the same call is correctly refused. Compounding this, src/core/shared/author.ts already resolves a per-invocation session id from CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID and six sibling harness keys, and src/core/history/history.ts writes agent_harness and agent_model but discards session — so the one signal that would separate two same-harness agents is detected and thrown away, leaving the completed history unable to attribute a mutation to an agent process either.\n\n- pm-w7mqzt (P2, under pm-ugqx) — the SDK documents how to write a package but does not supply what one needs. LIST_FILTER_FLAG_CONTRACTS is public and canonical; api.registerCommand takes FlagDefinition; the two shapes are unrelated and no adapter exists, so pm-calendar, pm-search-advanced and pm-governance-audit each hand-write the same filter table and have already drifted. Ten of fifteen first-party packages hand-roll the same PM_CLI_PACKAGE_ROOT loader. pm-command-kit is an exemplar, not a kit.\n\n- pm-ag9nka (P2, under pm-5oj5) — the built-in --profile timer starts inside the command handler, so it reports 200 ms for a 1,004 ms pm list and 286 ms for an 882 ms pm get. It is also documented nowhere. Every latency judgement or ratchet built on it measures a fraction, and least truthfully on the fastest commands.\n\n- pm-c5f0gh (P2, under pm-ugqx) — pm package init pm-demo-thing emits package name pm-pm-demo-thing and an invocation path of 'pm pm demo thing ping'. The prefixed form is the natural input, since every package in the ecosystem carries it. Exit 0, no warning.\n\n- pm-34w8za (P3, under pm-o2kc) — redact, repair and compact each re-implement the same load, verify, rewrite, re-anchor, report pipeline across 2,536 lines, with seven measured clone pairs reaching into cli copy and restore. Consolidation here is simplification with zero capability change, and it is the one surface where a silent divergence in re-anchoring is unrecoverable.\n\nRouted to existing owners as notes rather than new items: cross-file duplication census in four classes to pm-xspd; directory fan-out numbers (src/sdk 81 flat files, src/cli/commands 65) to pm-kb5h; the shipped research profile reproducing the literal-status-id half to pm-0mhspz; per-depth read cost 2.2 KB / 20 KB / 168 KB to pm-5t33or; the 2,037-item children scan behind a point read to pm-801d; the real-workspace bootstrap floor of 600-800 ms, which supersedes the empty-workspace 280 ms, to pm-yse5dt; the public-export-map dogfooding census to pm-quzx; the faucet at 287 to pm-wenq.\n\nVerified clean and not re-filed: type safety holds (strict true, two 'any' occurrences in non-spec source); the production dependency surface is seven packages; profile apply persists types, statuses, fields and workflows correctly into .agents/pm/schema and pm schema show-status reflects them; the built-in profiles lint clean; jscpd at the repository's own thresholds is genuinely zero.\n\nReading across the five: four of them are the same shape. A primitive exists and is correct — identity detection, the flag contract table, the timer, the scaffold — and the surface that consumes it either cannot reach it, discards part of it, or measures the wrong half. That is a different class from the missing-capability items that dominated earlier reviews, and it is the class that appears once a system is complete enough that the remaining defects are in the seams rather than in the parts."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T09:59:01.571Z"
    author: "harness:opencode"
    text: "## Brainstorm 2026-07-26 — post-ladder horizon sketch (beyond the six outcome milestones)\n\nThe ladder (pm-j82fd3 → pm-9rgaal → pm-w7ccmv → pm-o87av6 → pm-xp1xsw → pm-t4d7nz) ends 2028-06. Per policy pm-m08hza, new rungs are evidence-gated, so this note records *themes*, not commitments: each becomes a milestone proposal only when the rungs beneath it produce the enabling evidence. Date+theme identified (pm-zeifd2), never numbered.\n\n### Theme A — Organizational digital twins in production (post pm-t4d7nz)\nThe primitives land in the ladder: domain-pack kit (pm-g608fc), declarative policies (pm-mj42og), org roles story (pm-xc68), federation (pm-odohgb). The post-ladder outcome: a real organization runs its actual business logic — org structure, objectives, approvals, assets — on pm SDK primitives in production, with the twin-assets/twin-facilities storage (already in the on-disk layout) carrying live operational data. Success evidence: the VCS (pm-iiv1gl) and digital-twin-graph (pm-kr3t) exemplars plus one external domain-pack consumer unaffiliated with this repo.\n\n### Theme B — Inter-organization work networks (post Theme A)\nFederation across *trust boundaries*: companies link dependency graphs (a supplier's milestone blocks a customer's release) without sharing storage. Requires: portable attestation (pm-3z0k) as the trust primitive, verifiable authorship (pm-u14c), and merge semantics (pm-g5sx) generalized to partial replication. This is the \"supply chain of work\" — pm as the coordination fabric between organizations, not just within one.\n\n### Theme C — The context protocol as an open standard\npm's context-assembly contract (budgets pm-5t33or, projections pm-cxr0jb, delta reads pm-tuadp2, events stream) becomes a published, versioned protocol others implement — the way LSP standardized editor↔language-server. MCP is the transport; the *context grammar* is the standard. Success evidence: a third-party tracker or IDE implements the read side of the protocol against the open workspace format spec (pm-4jk8kx).\n\n### Theme D — Self-tuning workspaces\nThe learning loop (pm-uwfs feedback → pm-atfm/pm-kw4b eval gates) graduates from tuning context weights to tuning the workspace itself: schema drift proposals, workflow adjustments, and policy relaxations generated from usage evidence and approved through the declarative policy engine (pm-mj42og). The workspace becomes a continuously-improving system whose improvement proposals are themselves tracked items with immutable proof.\n\n### Theme E — Civilizational memory\nMulti-decade, million-item workspaces as institutional memory that outlives its authors: an agent onboards to a 30-year-old project in seconds because consolidation (pm-5qmm), readability windows (pm-e5yupa), and point-in-time projection (pm-5gf0xf) make every era queryable in bounded tokens. The philosophical end-state of project management = context management: no organization's accumulated context is ever lost, only layered.\n\n### Placement discipline\nNo items are filed from this note today except where a *primitive gap* is visible from inside the ladder. Each theme names its enabling evidence so a future review pass can promote or retire it with a decision item, keeping the roadmap append-only and speculation-free."
  - created_at: "2026-07-26T17:11:51.024Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "## Long-horizon structure beyond the outcome ladder (2026-07-26)\n\nThe six outcome milestones under this roadmap describe the horizon that ends at a universal domain platform. This note proposes what lies beyond it, so that long-range work has somewhere to attach without minting a seventh outcome milestone (the ladder is fixed; pm-m08hza owns promotion policy). Each horizon below names existing owners where they exist and states the gap where they do not. Nothing here is a new milestone — these are the themes a future promotion decision would draw from.\n\n**Horizon A — the format outlives the tool.** A tracker holding decades of institutional context is only durable if its bytes remain readable without its reader. pm-4jk8kx specifies the on-disk format and pm-e5yupa owns multi-decade readability. The unowned residue is stewardship and independent verification: who ratifies a format version, and what suite does a third-party implementation run to prove it reads and writes correctly. That is a conformance-and-governance question rather than an engineering one, and it becomes urgent only once an outside implementation exists — which is precisely why it should be specified before one does.\n\n**Horizon B — deletion without loss of proof.** Immutable history and statutory erasure are in direct tension over a multi-decade record. pm-h6wmtc now owns the reconciliation: retention classes, erasure resolution, and a proof that a redacted-then-compacted stream still verifies for a reader holding only the specification. This is the horizon that decides whether pm is adoptable in regulated settings at all.\n\n**Horizon C — federation with trust.** pm-odohgb owns cross-workspace references and remote dependency resolution; pm-is1a is the earlier federation lineage. The mechanics are filed; the trust model spanning them is not clearly separated — pm-u14c (authenticity) and pm-xc68 (authorization) are scoped to a single workspace, and a federated reference crossing an organisational boundary needs an answer for whose identity claims are believed and under what policy. Suggest this stays inside pm-odohgb until federation mechanics are accepted, then splits if the trust surface proves larger than the reference surface.\n\n**Horizon D — the fleet gains an objective function.** pm-t5dt4z measures cost, pm-gw6uyq measures outcomes per harness and model, pm-uey8od forecasts, pm-3oqjnc allocates. pm-2vs1za now owns the missing comparison — expected cost against expected value derived from recorded outcomes — so a fleet with more capacity than backlog can decline work rather than execute the queue because the queue exists. This is the difference between an autonomous tracker that compounds and one that churns.\n\n**Horizon E — context that survives its authors.** pm-vvib holds the cognitive-memory ADR and pm-4k6b the relevance engine, with pm-5qmm's epoch rollups shipped. The long-horizon question is reconstitution: an agent or person arriving with no prior exposure should be able to recover working context for a decade-old lineage from the tracker alone. The terminal-graph and release-attribution backfills completed today are the substrate for that; the retrieval side is owned by pm-4k6b and pm-p1g43q. What is not owned is the acceptance test — a reconstitution benchmark that measures how much of a closed epoch a cold reader can recover — and that is a natural extension of pm-kw4b rather than a new item.\n\n**Sequencing note.** A is a prerequisite for C, because federated references are worthless if the referenced format is not independently implementable. B is independent and gated on adoption pressure rather than engineering readiness. D depends on its four measurement inputs and on nothing else. E depends on the graph and attribution substrate, which is now in place."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T04:21:05.254Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Long-horizon structure, continued (2026-07-27). Extends the 2026-07-26 note that established horizons A through E; the outcome ladder stays fixed at six and pm-m08hza still owns promotion. Two further horizons, each named because the mechanism is owned and the horizon-level question above it is not.\n\nHorizon F — proof that does not require already holding the proof. pm-3z0k owns portable integrity attestation and its acceptance criteria are exactly right for what they cover: a detached bundle, a workspace rollup digest, standalone verification on a bare clone, and digest transitions that stay visible across compact, redact and repair. What that design proves is that a stream is self-consistent and that it has not diverged from a bundle the verifier already possesses. Over a multi-decade record the harder case is the verifier who possesses nothing. A hash chain living entirely inside the repository it describes is rewritable by anyone who can rewrite the repository, and a bundle exported from that repository inherits the same authority, so the proof reduces to whoever held an earlier copy. The unowned question is therefore where a root commits to the outside world: periodic anchoring of the rollup digest into something the project does not control, so that a party arriving cold in 2036 can establish that the 2026 record is the 2026 record without trusting the party who hands it over. The candidates are ordinary and boring, which is the point: a signed tag, a published digest in release metadata, a transparency log, a third-party timestamp. The design question is not which, it is what the anchoring cadence must be for the guarantee to mean anything, and what a verifier is entitled to conclude when the anchor is missing for a period. This should stay inside pm-3z0k until the bundle format is accepted, then split, because an anchoring policy over an unstable bundle format is premature.\n\nHorizon G — determinism as a global property, and its collision with automatic identity. pm-nfrhf0 states the environment entitlement well and its first acceptance criterion asks for episode workspaces created from a declared seed that produce byte-identical state across repeated runs, with identifiers, timestamps and ordering reproducible. That criterion is stronger than it looks, because reproducibility is not a feature one component provides; it is a property the whole system either has or does not. Every source of ambient nondeterminism must become injectable for it to hold: the clock that stamps history instants, the identifier generator, the ordering of concurrent writes, and identity.\n\nIdentity is where this becomes a genuine design tension rather than a task list, and it is worth stating at horizon level because the two commitments are both correct and they pull against each other. The project has a standing mandate that author identity is automatic and must stay automatic, so that agents need no environment variable and no flag, and the cluster under pm-03pq3o, pm-itsjf0 and pm-0zcwz6 is working to make that detection richer by capturing harness, model, reasoning level and session role. Detection reads ambient environment. A seeded episode requires that the same seed produce the same recorded author on any machine, under any harness, in any year. Those two requirements cannot both be satisfied by the same code path, and the resolution is not to weaken either: it is to recognise that detection and injection are two configurations of one identity resolver, that the mandate governs the interactive configuration, and that an episode declares its identity as part of its seed exactly as it declares its clock. Stating this now costs nothing and prevents the identity work from hardening around ambient reads in a way the environment work would later have to undo. The same argument applies to the clock and the identifier generator, which is why this is a horizon rather than three items.\n\nSequencing against the existing horizons. F is a successor to B, because a retention and erasure policy that mutates streams is only credible if the anchoring story survives the mutation, and B already owns the proof that a redacted-then-compacted stream still verifies. G is independent of A through F and depends only on the SDK-first work reaching the point where the runtime seams are injectable at all, which makes it a natural consumer of pm-usfg rather than a parallel effort."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T12:57:56.590Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Roadmap horizon H, recorded 2026-07-27, extending the horizon note that runs A through G. No seventh outcome milestone: promotion policy stays with pm-m08hza.\n\nHorizon H: the project is a set of ledgers, and nothing keeps them equal.\n\nThe seven horizons already recorded all ask what the record must be able to do at long range: stay readable (E), stay provable when parts are erased (B), stay trustworthy across federation boundaries (C), stay anchored to something outside itself (F), stay deterministic under an identity resolver that is deliberately automatic (G). Horizon H asks a prior question that turns out to be unanswered today: are the several records this project keeps even consistent with each other.\n\nThe measurement that produced this horizon, on 2026-07-27: CHANGELOG.md carries 69 version sections, git carries 77 release tags, and npm carries 70 published versions. Eighteen entries diverge, in both directions. Six artifacts installable from npm right now have no changelog section at all. Five versions the changelog announces were never published. Seven tags were declared and never delivered. Every control in the project measures one pair and none measures the closure, so the divergence accumulated silently over four months. That is the near-term defect, tracked by pm-q91qyd.\n\nThe horizon is what that generalizes to. A pm workspace intended to be authoritative for decades will keep more than one ledger by necessity, not by accident: the item store, the immutable history streams, the changelog, the release tags, the published artifacts, the attestations, and in a federated deployment the remote workspaces it references. Each is a projection of the same underlying events, produced at a different time by a different mechanism, and each can drift. The question horizon H asks is what invariant binds them, and whether that invariant is checkable rather than merely intended.\n\nThree properties this project does not yet have a position on.\n\nReconciliation as a first-class operation. Today reconciliation is an ad hoc script written by whoever noticed. It should be a declared relation between named ledgers with an explicit closure check, so that adding a ledger obliges someone to state how it reconciles rather than leaving it unbound. This is the generalization of pm-q91qyd from three release ledgers to all of them.\n\nDivergence classification. The three release divergences found today have different causes and different remedies, and lumping them into a count would have hidden that. Declared-not-delivered is an incident. Delivered-not-documented is a record gap that users can hit. Tagged-not-sectioned is bookkeeping. A general reconciliation contract must classify rather than count, which is the same lesson pm-2ler taught for dangling references and pm-j8z6 for linked-file drift.\n\nDirection of authority. When two ledgers disagree, which one wins, and does the answer depend on which pair. The history stream is the authority over the item store because the item store is derived from it. The registry is the authority over the changelog for what shipped, but the changelog is the authority over the registry for what was intended. Nothing states these, so every reconciliation to date has resolved them implicitly and differently.\n\nRelationship to the existing horizons: H is upstream of B and E. A record cannot be provably complete after erasure, or readable in twenty years, if the several projections of it were never required to agree in the first place."
  - created_at: "2026-07-27T13:01:51.972Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Ecosystem review pass 2026-07-27 (second of the day): release-ledger reconciliation, read-surface composability, and the end of the ordering-extraction approach.\n\nCorpus at the start: 2,131 items, 299 open, 1,819 closed, 13 canceled. Graph 2,134 nodes and 9,456 edges, one component, zero orphans, zero isolated or degree-one active nodes. Validate ok with six warning classes.\n\nThree items filed, each deduped against the full corpus and the never-re-file registry first.\n\npm-q91qyd, P1 under pm-u9d0. The project keeps three release ledgers and none of them agree. CHANGELOG.md has 69 version sections, git has 77 release tags, npm has 70 published versions, and 18 entries diverge in both directions. Six artifacts installable from npm right now (2026.5.3-5 through -8, 2026.5.4, 2026.5.5) have no changelog section at all. Five versions the changelog announces were never published. Seven tags were declared and never delivered. The reason nothing caught it is that every existing control measures a different pair: the CI changelog check proves the changelog matches the tracker, pm-7zs0 is forward-only and same-day, the publish verifier checks the artifact it just pushed. Nobody asks the closure question.\n\npm-sb0tns, P2 under pm-mpbb. Across the eleven read commands an agent or a shell pipeline uses, field projection reaches four, format selection six, streaming two, and one-row-per-line four. pm graph, with sixteen subcommands, has none of the four. Worse than the flag matrix, the row collection sits under a different key on every command: items, ready, ids, affected, groups, compact_activity, and scalar buckets on stats, so no single jq expression addresses a row on more than one surface. Scoped deliberately to projection and row identity, since streaming is pm-mav1ak, encoding grammar is pm-srns, the list-family projection split is pm-pjnu91, and zero-filled mode-paired envelopes are pm-cyrfjq.\n\npm-ye46ns, P3 under pm-gxs55a, executed this pass. See below.\n\nGraph work executed. Ten ordering edges applied from explicit precedence statements in durable item text: 3hps on 801d, 55ra on h3no, atfm and dfg0, 114v on 8t5x, 13bn on 7qjk, 9yfo on klo8, xspd on zuw8, bgcu on 2muu, hybj on iqgj. Twenty candidates dropped at a 67 percent refusal rate in three named classes, the dominant one being hub-note misattribution where a roadmap item's notes describe edges between other items. Corpus edges 9,456 to 9,505, blocked_by 391 to 403, one component and zero orphans preserved, graph audit unchanged at the same 11 info findings, no new cycles.\n\nThe finding that matters more than the edges: ten honest precedence statements exist in a 2,131-item corpus spanning months. Extraction over the existing text is exhausted, supersedes and discovered_from are saturated too, and the flat ordering graph is therefore an authoring gap rather than an extraction gap. The lever is capture at write time. Recorded as a comment on pm-lnrk so no later batch repeats the scan.\n\nCorrection recorded on pm-gxs55a. Its existing note says the reported critical path was wired entirely by the 2026-07-26 terminal backfill. The item files disagree: six of the seven chain edges carry 2026-03-07 timestamps and a maintainer-agent author, written minutes apart as each maintenance-loop run recorded its predecessor. The longest path in this workspace is a genuine sequential record, and it is the only one, which sharpens rather than softens the deficit. The same note records an ambiguity that is being retained rather than resolved: the 2026-03-07 edges and the 2026-04-04 dedupe comments assert opposite directions for the same pairs, and typing both would close a cycle. That is a design question for pm-gxs55a and pm-4020c5.\n\nExtended without new items. pm-csuce0 gained the ordering-inversion half with live evidence, since 2026.7.24-3 is a semver prerelease, sorts below 2026.7.24, and is excluded from every ordinary range, so the replacement is permanently unreachable to anyone on a caret range and two auto-releases reached npm on one day. pm-9yfo gained pm merge install as the third unaudited workspace mutation, and it is the severe one because it writes tracked .gitattributes and installs the merge drivers the whole multi-agent story depends on, with no record that it ran. pm-ei6x66 gained the observation that no git hook, husky config or lint-staged wiring exists, so every local gate is advisory. pm-4k6b gained catalog entry ordering-slack-conditioned-urgency, gated on the ordering graph becoming non-degenerate and required to prove it is a no-op until then. pm-e70zh5 gained the run-denominator caveat. pm-n7rr gained the 89-spelling census and the observation that package-owned commands are indistinguishable from core ones in help yet absent on a bare install.\n\nHygiene. Acceptance criteria written for pm-0d7ord, pm-a5w7vv and pm-hipfu9; estimates for those three and pm-rbg1qo. Validate warning classes six to four.\n\nVerified clean, not re-filed: npx and bunx both run the published 2026.7.27; today's scheduled auto-release succeeded in 49 seconds; the same-day guard is present and sound; validate ok with zero errors; one component and zero orphans hold after every write this pass."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T03:01:52.846Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Long-horizon structure, continued (2026-07-28). Extends the 2026-07-26 note establishing horizons A through E and the 2026-07-27 note adding F and G, alongside the ledger-reconciliation horizon H. The outcome ladder stays fixed at six and pm-m08hza still owns promotion. Two further horizons, each named because the mechanism beneath it is owned and the horizon-level question above it is not.\n\nHorizon I - correctness when some of the fleet is wrong. Every convergence guarantee the project has designed so far assumes the writers are honest and functioning. pm-2sbj8p weighs field-level merge against conflict-free replicated state and asks the right question for concurrency, but it asks it about agents that are all trying to be correct. pm-u14c establishes that an author is who they claim to be and pm-xc68 establishes what that author is permitted to do. None of the three covers the case the product is explicitly being built for: thousands of agents, some fraction of which are authenticated, authorized, and producing bad data. In a reinforcement-learning environment that fraction is not an edge case, it is the training signal - a policy under optimisation is expected to emit wrong actions, and the tracker is the substrate recording them. The same shape appears without adversaries whenever a misconfigured harness runs a loop.\n\nThe unowned question has three parts and they are separable. First, blast-radius bounding: what limits the damage one writer can do before anything notices, given that a mutation is immutable the moment it lands. Second, retroactive containment: how a run of entries is quarantined so that reads stop trusting them, without deleting them, because the record must stay complete and the quarantine decision must itself be recorded and reversible. Third, trust as a graded and earned property rather than a boolean, so that relevance, forecasting and work allocation can discount a writer whose recorded outcomes have been poor instead of treating every author as equally credible. That third part connects directly to the fleet objective function in horizon D, which currently assumes its inputs are trustworthy.\n\nThe reason this is a horizon and not an item is that a per-writer quarantine mechanism designed before the convergence model is settled would almost certainly be built on the wrong substrate. It should stay dormant until pm-2sbj8p is accepted, then be revisited, because conflict-free replicated state and three-way field merge give very different answers to what containing a writer even means.\n\nHorizon J - the ontology drifts under the record. The format horizon asks whether the bytes stay readable. This asks the harder question of whether they stay MEANINGFUL. A workspace declares its own types, statuses, fields and workflows, and the product's entire universality claim rests on that being freely customisable. Over a decade those declarations change: a status is retired, a type is split in two, a field changes what it measures. The items recorded under the old declarations are immutable and stay exactly as written. Nothing today records that the vocabulary they were written against no longer means what it meant.\n\nThis is not the same as pm-pae3wo, which proves that workspaces written by older and newer pm RELEASES stay readable and mergeable - that is the tool's schema, and it is versioned by the tool. It is not pm-e5yupa either, which is byte-level and format-level readability. It is the WORKSPACE's own ontology, authored by the user, versioned by nobody. A concrete instance already exists in this repository: the status occupancy census recorded on pm-pww8ax shows draft has never been used and blocked is effectively vestigial, so a future decision to retire either one would silently change the meaning of every historical item that names them in a status transition. The same argument applies with much more force to a custom field whose definition is edited rather than replaced.\n\nThe shape of the answer is probably an ontology version stamped on the workspace, a recorded migration whenever a declaration changes, and a read path that can interpret a historical item against the ontology in force when it was written rather than the one in force now. That last requirement is the expensive one and it is also the one that makes decade-old items genuinely legible instead of merely retrievable. pm-8jdc owns schema customization as a capability and is the natural home for the mechanism; what belongs at horizon level is the commitment that customisation implies a versioned ontology, because a product that lets users define meaning and never versions those definitions has a durability ceiling no amount of format work can raise.\n\nSequencing against the existing horizons. I is a successor to the convergence ADR and a prerequisite for D being trustworthy, since an objective function over unfiltered inputs optimises against noise. J is independent of A through I and gated only on schema customization maturing; it shares a boundary with A, since a format specification that does not carry an ontology version cannot express the migration J needs, which is an argument for settling A's version field before J is designed rather than after."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T10:04:49.422Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Horizon K - the record becomes the training signal, and the tracker enters the loop it measures.\n\nEvery horizon so far treats the record as a thing that is written and later read: A asks whether the bytes stay readable, F whether the proof travels, H whether the several ledgers agree, J whether the vocabulary still means what it meant. All of them assume the record describes work that happened for reasons outside the record.\n\nThat assumption is about to stop holding. pm-nfrhf0 states the entitlement: pm as the task substrate and the verifier for agent training and evaluation episodes, with reward computed from recorded state. pm-li2fj5 now owns the composition and pm-qd6gvf the trajectory the composition produces. If those ship, the workspace stops being only a description of work and becomes an environment: a thing agents are optimized against. Three questions follow that no current item asks, and that are worth stating before rather than after.\n\nFirst, the tracker becomes an optimization target. Once reward is computed from recorded state, an agent that maximizes reward is optimizing the record, not the work. Every metric this project already computes -- throughput and rework in pm-gw6uyq, forecast completion in pm-uey8od, measured value against cost in pm-2vs1za -- becomes a pressure surface the moment a policy is trained against it. The question is not whether to prevent that, which is not possible, but which recorded properties are cheap to satisfy without doing the work, and whether the ones a reward may read can be separated by construction from the ones it may not. pm-li2fj5 draws exactly this line for a single episode in its observable-versus-withheld field sets; the horizon question is whether the line generalizes past one episode to the tracker as a whole.\n\nSecond, observations need the same standing as mutations. The project has spent enormous effort making writes provable: hash-chained streams, portable attestation in pm-3z0k, per-entry signatures in pm-u14c. Reads have no equivalent. Nothing attests that a bounded context assembly was a faithful projection of the record at time T. That does not matter much when the consumer is a human reading a summary. It matters completely when the observation is a training input, because an unreproducible observation makes the entire trajectory unreproducible no matter how sound the mutation record underneath it is. Attested reads -- a context assembly that can be reproduced and shown to be exactly what the record supported at that moment -- is the read-side counterpart to a decade of write-side proof work, and nothing owns it.\n\nThird, the tool and the environment share a version. An agent trained against pm as it behaves in one release is trained against that release's context algorithm, its relevance scoring, its token budgets, its degradation ladder. Change any of them and every recorded trajectory silently refers to an environment that no longer exists. This is the format-readability question of Horizon A asked about behaviour rather than bytes: pm-4jk8kx makes the on-disk format independently implementable, but two conforming implementations with different relevance scoring are the same format and a different environment. Whether behavioural versioning is required, and at what granularity, is open.\n\nThe shape of the horizon: pm has spent its whole life making the record trustworthy for people and for agents reading it. This asks what changes when the record is also what agents are shaped by -- and the honest answer is that at least one guarantee the project is proud of, the provability of writes, turns out to cover only half of what a training loop needs."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T10:09:09.970Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Review pass 2026-07-28, ecosystem planning and simplification brainstorm.\n\nMethod that produced everything here: compare a declared number against the measured one on the real workspace, then read the code that is supposed to enforce the difference. Every finding below is a declared-versus-observed gap, not a code smell.\n\nToken efficiency. The contracts table declares a default output ceiling per command and nothing binds it. Measured at HEAD on this tracker of 2,154 items and 40,446 history entries: activity 1,591,219 tokens against a declared 4,000, deps 46,498 against 4,000, health 33,178 against 6,000, list 14,234 against 4,000, events and duplicates roughly 2.3x each, validate exactly at its ceiling. Seven of eighteen sampled surfaces over, and the overruns are all surfaces whose size is a function of workspace scale. pm-z2j1qt owns activity, which has limit null and returns the entire immutable record on a bare invocation. The census now lives in the description of pm-5t33or, which previously had acceptance criteria and no description.\n\nWhy none of that was caught: both gates named after tokens are structurally unable to fail, which is pm-9sui7t. token-budget-gate.mjs seeds three items into a temp workspace, measures seventeen surfaces, and compares them against budgets that are recorded observations of that three-item fixture rather than against the contract ceiling, which it never reads; its corpus omits every overrunning surface; and it runs only in nightly and release, not on pull requests. The other gate, agent-token-surface-baseline.json, measures only help, contracts and MCP payload sizes -- discovery cost, not answer cost. pm list is 649 bytes on the fixture and 56,937 bytes here, 87.7x, and no check anywhere can see it. Same class as pm-lpqln4: a gate whose passing carries no information.\n\nIdentity. Harness and effort resolve correctly. Model does not and structurally cannot, which is pm-ffz0a9. HarnessSignalDescriptor can express only environment keys, so a dimension published through any other channel is inexpressible rather than merely undetected. claude-code declares ANTHROPIC_MODEL and CLAUDE_MODEL as its model sources and sets neither; it does set CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID, which pm already reads, and the session record that variable names carries the model on every assistant entry -- 62 records reading claude-opus-5 in the session that produced this note, plus harness version 2.1.220. A live sandbox mutation at HEAD records harness claude-code, effort xhigh from environment, and model null. Corpus agrees: 900 explicit provenance entries, zero observed models across 6,236 harness-attributed entries. This is why pm-0zcwz6 closed correctly and changed nothing -- it added three more instances of the one channel the harness does not use for this dimension.\n\nEnvironment substrate. pm-nfrhf0 names four requirements and says plainly that the composition is what is missing. The four now have owners; the composition did not. pm-li2fj5 owns the portable episode specification and the SDK control surface, pm-qd6gvf owns the trajectory projection that training, offline evaluation and fleet analytics all consume. The distinction that matters: reward must be a function of recorded state, and grading fields must be unreachable by projection rather than by convention.\n\nRelease clock. Today's scheduled run correctly reported release_already_cut_today and did not cut a second version; npx and bunx both resolve 2026.7.28. The one-per-day guarantee held today for the reason pm-ki67py already documents -- incidentally, because no out-of-order tag exists yet.\n\nGraph. All-status degree census attached to pm-6olc95: zero isolates at any status, 229 closed items at degree 2 or below, and only 8 closed items with no typed dependency edge at all. Those 8 were inspected individually and repaired where the item's own resolution supplied the evidence. Extraction remains exhausted; this was connectivity repair on named outliers, not another scan.\n\nTwo traps recorded while working. graph descendants rejects semantic edge kinds, so an item with implements children reports zero descendants while graph impact reports thirty -- pm-3dyec2, reproduced. And update --dep appends without idempotence while dep-remove's id+kind selector removes every match, so collapsing a duplicate row means deleting the edge and re-adding it, which is pm-flnefm."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T10:34:23.922Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "## Horizon L — the inventory/implementation split (2026-07-28)\n\nRecorded as a theme, not a commitment, per pm-m08hza.\n\nThis pass measured the cost of pm's own extensibility and found the two headline goals — \"extremely extensible\" and \"everything feels instant\" — are not merely in tension, they are structurally opposed by one design choice: **the only cheap description of what an extension contributes is its coarse `capabilities[]` array; everything precise lives inside the module and costs an import to read.**\n\nThe evidence chain (pm-j0w7j9, pm-021kdp):\n\n- `extensionNeedsActivationForProbe` evaluates the `{hooks, parser, preflight, renderers}` tier *before* the \"declared scope did not match, skip\" exit. So an extension that declares a narrow `activation.commands` scope is still imported for every command if it also declares any of those four capabilities. The shipped `--capability renderers` scaffold emits exactly that combination — a declared scope that the runtime ignores.\n- Measured 2.00x at HEAD (784ms vs 392ms, 14 extensions). pm-4oww closed on a ≤1.5x criterion; it is violated and nothing gates it.\n- `pm extension --install` already computes the precise inventory (`registered_actions`) by loading the extension once, then writes only `capabilities[]` to `.managed-extensions.json` and discards the rest.\n- `composeExtension(blueprint)` already describes every surface precisely — but as a value inside the module, so reading it costs the import the gate is trying to avoid.\n\nThe generalizable claim: **a system that wants both unbounded extension and bounded startup must be able to answer \"what does this contribute?\" without executing the thing that contributes it.** Every workaround short of that — capability tiers, conservative fallbacks, per-capability special cases like the schema decision in pm-halx — is a heuristic standing in for an inventory that was computed and thrown away.\n\nWhere this points beyond the immediate fix:\n\n- **Contribution inventory as a first-class artifact.** Install-time snapshot, verified against the module on install/update, authoritative for registry, `--help`, unknown-command suggestions, MCP tool enumeration, and contracts. Command surfaces become enumerable statically — which is the same underlying gap pm-6j7r1a records for the 89-vs-75 help/contracts divergence.\n- **A second-order property worth naming:** once contributions are data, an agent can be told what an extension does without any of it running. That is the extensibility half of \"project management = context management\" — the surface an agent must reason about becomes readable rather than executable.\n- **Fence:** this is not a new perf item and not a new extensions epic. pm-j0w7j9 owns the defect, pm-021kdp owns the architecture, both under pm-ugqx. Do not file per-capability activation items; cite pm-j0w7j9."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T15:05:22.492Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "## Horizon M — one fact, many readers, no proof they agree (2026-07-28)\n\nRecorded as a theme, not a commitment, per pm-m08hza. Extends the horizon set A through L.\n\nEvery horizon so far asks whether the record survives: A whether the bytes stay readable, F whether the proof travels, H whether the several ledgers agree with each other, J whether the vocabulary still means what it meant, K what happens when the record becomes a training target. All of them treat a stored fact as having one meaning that is either preserved or lost.\n\nThis pass found a stored fact with two meanings at the same instant, in the same version, on the same disk. A prerequisite between two items can be written as a blocked_by row on the dependent or as a blocks row on the blocker. The graph engine reads both and orders them identically. Blocker resolution in src/core/item/actionability.ts reads only the first and reports the second as no constraint at all. Writing both, which is what an author does on discovering the split, makes validate report a dependency cycle. Three ways to state one fact, three different answers, none of them flagged at write time. 219 blocks rows exist against 127 declared blocked_by rows, so the reading the scheduler cannot see is the more common one, and the v1 milestone chain uses the same kind in two opposite senses inside single items.\n\nThe general shape is what makes it a horizon rather than a defect. This project has invested heavily in single-sourcing declarations — contract tables, shared enum constants, one flag lexicon, one actionability definition shared by next, context and list-blocked. It has no notion of consumer conformance: a proof that every reader of a stored field derives the same meaning from it. Declarations are single-sourced; interpretations are not.\n\nThat gap scales badly, in a way the roadmap already commits to making worse. The readers of an item's dependency rows today are the CLI, the MCP surface, the public SDK, the graph engine, the validate checks, the changelog generator and the relevance engine. The outcome ladder adds more: pm-qd6gvf projects the record as an observation-action-outcome trajectory, pm-li2fj5 composes episodes from it, pm-8bgeye attests what a read consumed, pm-2vs1za computes work economics over it, and horizon K makes reward a function of recorded state. Pairwise disagreement between readers grows quadratically while each individual reader stays internally consistent and therefore passes its own tests. A reward computed from a field one reader interprets differently is not merely wrong, it is wrong in a direction no test can see, because both readers are correct about their own semantics.\n\nThree questions worth stating before rather than after.\n\nFirst, what is the unit of meaning that gets single-sourced? The candidate is not the field and not the kind but the relation: an ordering relation has exactly one direction, and every spelling accepted at the write path canonicalizes into it. This is the direction pm-4020c5 is already pointed and pm-jkbqt8 now depends on, so the first instance has an owner. The horizon question is whether the same discipline applies to every stored relation, or only to the two that happened to be caught.\n\nSecond, what does a conformance proof look like when the readers are in different packages and languages? pm-e6tm5c freezes the SDK export surface and pm-pae3wo runs a cross-version matrix, but both check shape, not interpretation. A property that says every reader of relation R returns the same answer for the same corpus needs the readers to be enumerable, which today they are not — an extension can read dependency rows directly and no registry knows it did.\n\nThird, how does an interpretation change get released? Canonicalizing a spelling changes what the existing corpus means. Horizon A asks whether old bytes are readable and J whether old vocabulary still resolves; this asks whether a corpus written under one interpretation can be re-read under another without silently changing recorded history. The 219 rows and the twelve inverted v1 milestone edges are the first migration, small enough to be a rehearsal and already carrying a documented refusal to do it blindly on pm-1w6scp.\n\nThe near-term thread is narrow and already owned: pm-4020c5 rules on direction, pm-jkbqt8 makes the readers agree for ordering, pm-1w6scp records the legacy disposition, and pm-6x7o's governance audit is the natural home for a check that fails when an accepted spelling is unreachable by a consumer. The horizon is the generalization, and it belongs to whichever epic eventually owns interpretation as a first-class artifact rather than a property of whoever wrote the reader."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T22:37:52.293Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Three further horizons, recorded 2026-07-28, extending the horizon note that runs A through M. No seventh outcome milestone: the ladder stays fixed at six and promotion policy stays with pm-m08hza. Each is named for the mechanism beneath it rather than for a feature, and each is grounded in something measured this pass rather than anticipated.\n\nHORIZON N - the name is not a name. Every guarantee this project has designed is stated about an id. A version belongs to an id, a chain verifies an id, a merge reconciles an id, an edge connects two ids, a release attributes an id. Horizon N asks the prior question that turns out to be unanswered: is an id a stable, unique, permanent name for one thing.\n\nToday it is not, and the failure is not theoretical. pm-e3gn0z proves that deleting an item makes its id immediately re-mintable while its history stream is deliberately retained, so one immutable stream can hold two unrelated items - and pm history --verify, pm validate --check-storage-integrity, and pm health all report success on the result, while point-in-time reads answer two different questions under one id with full stated confidence. That is one mechanism. There are three more. Ids are minted from a probe that can only see the local working tree, so two branches can independently mint the same id and the only defence is post-merge detection rather than prevention. The workspace id prefix can be renamed, which pm-kl19 owns, and a rename is an identity migration over a record that cannot be rewritten. And identity in the other direction is equally soft: the mandated automatic author detection collapses every process of one harness to one author string, which pm-z8qd4k owns, so the writer of an entry is not uniquely named either.\n\nWhat makes this a horizon rather than a defect list is that the four mechanisms are the same shape. In each case a name is treated as a primary key by every consumer while being minted, reused, migrated, or collapsed by some producer that never had to promise uniqueness. Horizons A and J ask whether old bytes stay readable and whether old vocabulary still resolves. N asks whether the thing the bytes are about is still the same thing. It is upstream of B, because erasure needs to know what it is erasing; of F, because a portable attestation attests about ids; and of M, because two readers cannot be proven to agree about a fact whose subject is ambiguous. The candidate invariant is stateable: an id, once recorded in any durable stream, is never reissued in any branch, and every guarantee elsewhere may assume it.\n\nHORIZON O - the fleet pays for orientation and no one is counting. Every budget this project has designed is per invocation. pm-5t33or declares per-command ceilings, pm-t5dt4z measures per-operation cost, pm-cha95z bounds diagnostics. All three bound one answer for one caller. Nothing bounds the aggregate, and at the scale the roadmap targets the aggregate is the whole cost.\n\nMeasured this pass: the four reads AGENTS.md mandates for orientation cost about 6,095 tokens on this 2,176-item workspace, and almost all of that is a function of workspace state rather than of who is asking. A thousand agents orienting on the same repository compute the same ranking a thousand times. The marginal agent contributes almost no information to the assembly and pays full price for it, so the economics invert precisely as the fleet grows - the regime the product is being built for is the regime where the current design is least efficient. pm-tch9cs now owns the mechanism: attested, content-addressed shared assemblies plus a fleet-level budget, so the shared part is computed once and each agent pays only its own delta.\n\nThe horizon is larger than the cache. It is that cost becomes the binding constraint before scale does, and the project has no unit of account for it. There is no declarable fleet target, no observable aggregate, and no way to answer whether adding the thousand-and-first agent is worth what it costs. Horizon D gives the fleet an objective function over the VALUE of work; O is the other half of the same ledger, the cost of knowing what the work is. Neither is decidable without the other, and D currently assumes its inputs are free.\n\nHORIZON P - the record has no provisional tier. Every write is permanent proof the moment it lands. That is the correct default for delivery and the wrong default for exploration, and the corpus shows the gap has never been closed rather than never been noticed: the draft status exists in the schema and has been written zero times in 40,856 history entries.\n\nThe consequence binds exactly where the roadmap is heading. An episodic harness - training, evaluation, benchmarking, sim-to-real - explores by construction. It creates candidates, tries orderings, and records observations that turn out to be wrong. Today it can write into the live workspace, where every discarded hypothesis becomes permanent proof indistinguishable from delivered work and the relevance engine will score it forever; or it can snapshot and restore the whole workspace, which pm-dkrmzv delivered, but whole-workspace rollback destroys concurrent writers and is therefore unusable on a shared tracker. pm-xpzped now owns the per-item provisional tier that coexists with permanent work in one live workspace.\n\nThe hard part, and the reason this is a horizon, is that discard must not weaken the integrity guarantee. Provisional entries are real history; what a discard changes is their standing, not their existence. So the project needs a notion of recorded-but-not-binding that is itself verifiable - a second grade of proof rather than an absence of proof. That is a different question from B, which asks how to delete under statutory compulsion without breaking a chain, and from K, which asks what happens when the record becomes the training signal. P asks whether a record that can only ever accumulate binding claims can host exploration at all, and if not, what the second grade is and who is allowed to promote across it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-28T22:45:55.323Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Review pass 2026-07-28 (identity, storage shape, contract coverage, and the origin epoch). Recorded here rather than as an item, per the standing rule.\n\nMethod that produced the findings, worth reusing: take a primitive the project shipped THIS WEEK and ask what invariant it silently depends on. The reproducible-execution primitives from pm-rbcvt2 make a minted id a function of a seed, which turns a probabilistic hazard into a deterministic test - and that immediately proved that a deleted item's id is re-mintable onto its retained history stream. The same lens on the workspace snapshot command showed a rendered command family with no contract entry at all. Neither finding needed a new probe axis; both needed a new primitive to be pointed at the old guarantees.\n\nSix items filed, none duplicating an existing owner.\n\n  pm-e3gn0z  P1  a deleted item's id is immediately re-mintable, so one immutable stream can hold two unrelated items while history --verify, validate --check-storage-integrity and health all report success, and point-in-time reads answer two different questions under one id\n  pm-v3f1n4  P1  the same dependency list has two live on-disk encodings chosen by writer path, 2,149 tabular against 32 expanded, so null is indistinguishable from absent, byte-reproducibility is defeated, and a branch merge sees a whole-block rewrite\n  pm-vk7zek  P1  the child hierarchy kind has an unenforced direction and cardinality; 7 of 15 rows are inverted against the product's own declaration and no channel, validator or audit finding notices\n  pm-1jrdri  P2  the rendered command surface is not a subset of the contracted surface; pm workspace ships in help, runs, and exits 2 on its own contract lookup\n  pm-xpzped  P2  provisional write tier, so exploration can be recorded without becoming binding proof - the draft status has never been written once in 40,856 entries\n  pm-tch9cs  P2  fleet-amortized context assembly, so a thousand agents asking one question pay for one computation instead of a thousand\n\nPlanning artifacts recorded against existing owners, no new items.\n\n  pm-pbyu   the complete placement table - all 85 rendered spellings mapped to twelve nouns, fourteen permanent bare-verb aliases named, five open rulings stated. The ADR is now a ruling on a table rather than on a principle.\n  pm-5t33or measured cost of the mandated agent loop, about 6,095 tokens of orientation, plus the five-rung degradation ladder and its two honesty properties\n  pm-4k6b   two catalog entries by slug - refuted-and-abandoned-evidence-retrieval and trust-weighted-context-assembly; slug ledger updated on pm-g8t7b9\n  pm-lnrk   terminal-lineage state, with the both-encodings parser correction\n\nGraph. The origin epoch is reachable for the first time: the seven v1 delivery milestones gained the scalar parent their dependency rows had already asserted, taking pm-j7a from 3 hierarchy descendants to 70. Every one of the 1,868 terminal items now carries at least one typed semantic outbound edge, zero remaining. Edges 9,767 to 9,818, nodes 2,179 to 2,185, cycles unchanged at 8, isolated and degree-one active nodes both zero, audit findings unchanged at 11 info. Hierarchy depth distribution is 178 roots, 374 at depth 1, 1,255 at 2, 335 at 3, 33 at 4, 6 at 5, 1 at 6 - a wide forest of shallow programme trees, which is what the project's actual history looks like, so the lever for traversability is typed-edge completeness and epoch partitioning rather than invented depth.\n\nVerified working, not to be re-derived: npx and bunx both run published 2026.7.28 through a real init-create-read sequence under bun 1.3.11; four successful Auto Release runs on the UTC day produced exactly one published version, which is the same-day guard behaving correctly; automatic identity records harness, instance and effort xhigh from environment with no PM_AUTHOR, leaving only model and session role absent as pm-ffz0a9 and pm-itsjf0 already state; EPIPE handling is clean, pm list-all piped into head exits 0 with empty stderr; validate reports one warning class, the 291 closed items missing structured resolution that pm-e9zh owns.\n\nThree horizons added, N through P, recorded separately: the name is not a name, the fleet pays for orientation and no one is counting, and the record has no provisional tier."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T04:53:54.699Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Horizon Q - every number in the product was fitted to one corpus, and nothing says which of them travel (2026-07-29)\n\nThe claim this project makes. The tool is universal: it must serve a scratch workspace of a few items spanning minutes and an archive of millions spanning decades, and it must be customizable to domains that are not software project management at all. Two outcome milestones already assert versions of this, one for proven scale and one for the universal domain platform.\n\nThe prior question neither of them asks. Every threshold, default, cap and heuristic currently in the product was chosen while looking at exactly one corpus: this repository, 2,186 items, one team, seven months, one language, one release cadence. Some of those numbers are principled and will hold anywhere. Some are artifacts of this corpus and will be wrong the first time the tool meets a different one. Nothing in the product, the contracts, the documentation or the tracker states which is which.\n\nThe population is not small and it is not hypothetical. A file-list worklist truncates at 40. A graph centrality query bounds at 10. A next-recommendation slate is 5 rows. Default token budgets, recency half-lives and decay constants are chosen numbers. A static quality gate caps a file at 3,400 non-blank non-comment lines. Duplicate detection uses a jaccard threshold and a similarity score of 0.99 for an issue-code match. Search relevance is graded against a curated golden-query set drawn from this project's own vocabulary. The observation that hierarchy depth maxes at 6 has already been recorded, correctly, as a fact about this corpus that must never be manufactured elsewhere - which is precisely the discipline this horizon asks be applied to everything else.\n\nWhy it matters more as the tool succeeds. A constant fitted to this corpus is not merely suboptimal elsewhere; it is silently suboptimal, because the same output shape appears and nothing signals that a bound was chosen for a different population. A 40-row worklist that samples 3 percent of a corpus reads exactly like one that samples 100 percent. A half-life tuned to a seven-month project makes a decade-old archive look uniformly stale. A golden-query set drawn from one vocabulary certifies retrieval quality for that vocabulary only. Each of those is a correct engineering decision today and a latent wrong answer at the scale the roadmap promises.\n\nWhat the horizon asks for, in order. First, an inventory: every numeric constant that affects behaviour, declared as data with its unit, its provenance, and one of three labels - invariant, meaning it is derived from a stated principle and does not depend on the corpus; calibrated, meaning it was fitted and the fitting procedure is recorded and repeatable; or arbitrary, meaning it was chosen and nobody has checked. Arbitrary is an honest label and most of the population will start there. Second, a rule that arbitrary constants may not be load-bearing on a correctness claim, only on a presentation default. Third, for every calibrated constant, the corpus it was calibrated against must be nameable, and re-calibrating against a different corpus must be an ordinary operation rather than a code change. Fourth, a spectrum conformance suite that runs the same behavioural assertions against corpora at three or four orders of magnitude and reports which constants changed their answer.\n\nRelation to the horizons already recorded. Horizon A asks whether old bytes stay readable and J whether old vocabulary still resolves; this asks whether the tool's own tuning stays valid as the population changes, which is the same species of question aimed at behaviour rather than at format or ontology. Horizon G asks for determinism as a global property; a calibrated constant is a hidden input to determinism, and an undeclared one makes two installations diverge for reasons neither can see. Horizon O observes that the fleet pays for orientation and nobody counts; this observes that the fleet's orientation is priced by numbers nobody has justified.\n\nThe cheap first step, available now. Nothing here requires the scale milestone to land. The inventory is a grep and a judgement call per constant, and its output is a table that can be committed and gated. The gate is that a new numeric literal in a behavioural path must arrive with a label. That single rule stops the population growing while the existing members are dispositioned, which is the same shape as every other ratchet this project has used successfully."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T16:59:13.819Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Horizon R - every guarantee this project makes is verified by the party that wrote it, against the corpus that produced it (2026-07-29)\n\nThe claim. The product is universal: it must serve any project, any domain, a scratch workspace of minutes and an archive of decades, a single author and thousands of concurrent agents. Two outcome milestones assert versions of that, one for proven scale and one for the universal domain platform, and a third freezes a stability contract on top of both.\n\nWhat all of the supporting evidence has in common. The corpus is this repository - 2,193 items, seven months, one team, one language, one release cadence. The retrieval golden set is drawn from this project's own vocabulary. The harness identity detector is verified by reading entries written by the harness that wrote the detector. The merge conformance branches are branches this repository made. The domain packs that demonstrate universality are exemplars this repository authored. The output token budgets are measured on this tracker. The million-item fixture is produced by a script in this repository, in this repository's shape. Even the automatic provenance claim is confirmed by inspecting history entries this session created. Every one of those is a legitimate engineering control and not one of them is an independent check.\n\nHow this differs from the two adjacent horizons already recorded. Horizon Q asks which of the product's fitted constants travel to another population - it is about the parameters. This horizon asks who could tell us - it is about the oracle. Horizon F asks for external anchoring of the hash chain, a third party for the record. This asks for external validation of behaviour, a third party for the claims. Q and F can both be satisfied by building something. This one cannot.\n\nWhy it matters more as the tool succeeds. Self-verification has a ceiling that added rigour does not raise. More gates, more property tests, more fuzzing and more negative controls all still run against inputs the same authors imagined, so they extend coverage inside a boundary rather than moving it. The first genuinely foreign corpus, the first harness the maintainers do not run and the first domain that is not software project management will each surface a class of defect that no internal gate could have generated. The moment to learn that is before a stability contract freezes the command grammar, the SDK surface and the storage format, because after the freeze the same discovery costs a migration.\n\nWhat the horizon asks for, in order. First, one corpus the maintainers did not create, imported through the interop adapters, with its measured shape published beside this repository's shape so the difference is a number rather than an impression. Second, one harness the maintainers do not run, exercised end to end, so identity detection, token budgets and error envelopes are read by something with different assumptions. Third, one domain that is not software project management, authored by someone outside the project against the domain-pack kit, with the friction recorded as items rather than resolved in private conversation. Fourth, a standing channel so that foreign corpora and foreign harnesses are a recurring input rather than a single event.\n\nWhat it does not ask for. Not a public launch and not a community program - those belong to the positioning epic. Not federation across workspaces - that is its own horizon. Not more internal testing. One instance of each of the three kinds is enough to start, and the value is almost entirely in the first instance of each.\n\nThe fence that makes this a horizon rather than an item. It is satisfied only by evidence originating outside the project, so it cannot be completed by building anything, and it is the only horizon whose completion is not under this project's control. That is the reason to name it before the general-availability gate rather than after: the gate can then state honestly whether it is freezing a contract that has been checked from outside or one that has only ever been checked from inside.\n\nConsumers. The general-availability stability milestone and the universal-domain milestone both depend on an answer here. The interop adapters and the domain-pack kit are the mechanisms. The cross-version compatibility matrix is the closest existing control and covers versions of ourselves only. The constants inventory that Horizon Q asks for becomes far more valuable once there is a second population to compare against."
  - created_at: "2026-07-29T21:26:10.324Z"
    author: "harness:codex"
    text: "Ecosystem planning synthesis — 2026-07-29 live state\n\nThe complete tracker contains 2,197 items: 308 open, 1,876 closed, 13 canceled, and no claimed or in-progress work at census time. All 2,197 current records and all 41,391 history events were inspected through pm read surfaces. Hash-chain validation reports zero drift. The graph is one connected component with 2,200 nodes including three historical missing targets, 9,936 edges before this enrichment, zero active isolates, and zero active nodes of degree one or less. Exact and parent-scope duplicate checks found no actionable active duplicate. Fuzzy candidates were inspected as parent/child decomposition, recurrence, verification, or distinct defects; no new work item was created.\n\nRoadmap sequencing is now explicit rather than a flat list: the noun-verb ADR governs the alias foundation; consolidation slices feed the agent-legibility story and milestone; the SDK-first epic feeds SDK completion; the scale-out epic feeds proven scale; provable history, agent legibility, SDK completion, and scale feed the GA contract; GA, scale, SDK, history, and customization feed the universal-domain outcome. This creates a long, typed, explainable strategic path without inventing artificial hierarchy.\n\nPlanning principle: depth is not a KPI. A truthful wide program can be shallow in parent hierarchy while being rich in typed causal, verification, implementation, discovery, supersession, and evidence paths. The quality invariant is that every item reaches an outcome through bounded, provenance-carrying typed paths and that every inferred edge can cite durable evidence. pm-bzmeaa owns the invariant; pm-s5e04g owns evidence-derived reconstruction; pm-lnrk owns terminal-history backfill; pm-hnc9w7 owns legacy graph hygiene.\n\nCurrent risk ledger is already canonical. The 292 terminal resolution-metadata warnings remain owned by pm-e9zh. The 11 graph-audit findings are informational legacy debt owned by pm-hnc9w7. Automatic provenance still lacks model values across the current history corpus and is owned by pm-ffz0a9, pm-rbg1qo, pm-v8gfi7, and pm-3yxwv5. Published npm, npx, and bunx 2026.7.29 work live, but the false-positive Bun release gate remains open under pm-lpqln4. No duplicate item was filed for any of these."
    edited_at: "2026-07-29T21:27:44.850Z"
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T09:24:13.602Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Roadmap Horizon S — surfaces multiply faster than the contract meant to unify them, and governance cannot catch up because it is applied after the fact\n\nMeasured 2026-07-30. Ask the product the same question three times and get three answers. How many things can pm do? pm --help renders 85 top-level spellings. The published contract table PM_CORE_COMMAND_NAMES declares 70. The MCP server answers 86 through the pm_run action enum plus 28 narrow tools plus two directly wired tools. The three sets are not nested and differ in both directions: 26 action spellings exist only in MCP, and 7 real capability families exist only in the terminal, among them the entire multi-agent merge integration, the whole content-addressed workspace snapshot and restore primitive, and the retrieval evaluation surface.\n\nThis is not a defect list. It is a growth law. Every capability wave adds a binding for the new surface it needs and never removes an older binding, because nothing makes the older binding impossible to write. Two MCP waves have already shipped and closed - one deriving per-tool input schemas from flag contracts, one warning on silently dropped nested options - and neither touched the action name set, because neither had to. Governance that inspects a surface after it exists can only ever report drift; it cannot prevent the next surface from being hand-written.\n\nThe horizon question: what would make a hand-written agent-facing surface impossible rather than merely discouraged? The answer is generative closure. One declaration is the only path through which any surface can be produced - CLI registration, MCP tool set, completions, docs, and the SDK action list are all emitted, never authored - and the build fails not when the surfaces disagree but when a surface exists that was not emitted. That inverts the current control direction: today a gate compares two hand-maintained artifacts and reports a difference; under closure there is only one artifact and the comparison is unnecessary by construction.\n\nThree things this horizon is NOT, so it does not absorb existing owners. It is not the command-grammar conformance gate, which stops the CLI noun set regrowing within one surface. It is not single-source surface tiering, which decides what each audience sees of one declared surface. It is not the SDK export-surface freeze, which pins a shape once chosen. All three assume the surface is authored and then governed. This horizon asks whether authoring a surface should be possible at all.\n\nWhy it belongs on the long horizon rather than in a slice. Generative closure is only affordable once the declaration is complete enough to emit from, which is the SDK-complete outcome, and only provable once there is a gate registry with negative controls that can assert the absence of an unemitted surface. It therefore sits downstream of SDK completeness and upstream of the GA stability contract: freezing a grammar that is still hand-authored in three places freezes the divergence too.\n\nSame-day corroboration that the pattern is general and not an MCP accident: the read-intent layer shipped the same week declares a per-intent token budget and writes it into an option key that three of its five commands do not accept, and the paired dependency flags accept different value grammars for the same selector so one refuses what the other silently ignores. In all three cases a declaration existed and no single boundary was responsible for making it true."
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T17:12:41.312Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Long-horizon structure, 2026-07-30 (evening) — horizon T: declarations are scalars where they must be functions of the corpus they will be enforced against\n\nRecorded after pointing the context-budget bundle that shipped this afternoon at its own declarations. Horizons A through S stand unchanged; this extends the ladder rather than reopening it.\n\nWhat the day proved. Enforcement of declared token budgets landed and works: the budget is applied at one egress point, a receipt is emitted, and an overrunning payload is refused. Enforcement then immediately falsified the declarations it enforces. On the live 2,215-item tracker three of five built-in intents cannot emit any result at all, because the declared ceiling is below the smallest projection the command can produce: next execute declares 1200 tokens against a measured floor of 1438, context orient declares 2400 against 8417, context handoff declares 2200 and behaves the same. Run the identical five intents against a two-item workspace and every one of them fits comfortably. The numbers were therefore correct at the moment and the scale at which they were authored, and became wrong as the corpus they govern grew past the corpus they were measured on. Nothing in the build noticed, because nothing compares a declared constant against the corpus it will be enforced against.\n\nThe generalisation. This project keeps discovering the same shape and keeps treating each instance as its own defect. A declared quantity is authored once, as a scalar, against whatever corpus the author had at hand, and is then enforced forever. The token budgets are today's instance. The 2,048-event retention on the context usage ledger is another: it is a count, while the compaction trigger that follows it is a byte threshold, so on this corpus the trigger fires on every read and the count-based policy can never satisfy it. The 200-candidate cap on one serving surface next to an uncapped one-row-per-open-item serving surface on its sibling is a third. The pattern is not miscalibration. It is that the declaration language has no way to say what a number depends on.\n\nHorizon T, therefore: every declared numeric bound in the product is expressed as a function of the corpus dimension it depends on, and the build instantiates it at no fewer than two corpus scales before accepting it. A ceiling on output tokens is a function of the projected row width and the requested row count. A retention bound is a function of entry size, and its trigger is expressed in the same unit as its policy. A candidate cap is a function of the candidate population, or it is declared as a sampling decision with a stated loss. Where a constant is genuinely correct at all scales, that must be an assertion the build checks rather than an assumption the author held. The instrument that makes this checkable is the scale corpus generator, so horizon T is downstream of horizon Q and cannot be closed before it: a declaration cannot be validated at two scales while only one corpus shape exists.\n\nWhat horizon T is not. It is not horizon Q, which is about the generator producing corpora that do not resemble real ones; T is about the declarations validated against them. It is not the output-budget contract itself, which is an open feature and the place the first instance will be fixed. It is not the proven-scale outcome milestone, which publishes numbers that were measured; T is about numbers that were asserted. And it is explicitly not a request to make every constant configurable: a configurable wrong number is still a wrong number, and the failure mode here was silence, not rigidity.\n\nFalsifier. If a systematic sweep of the declared numeric constants in the product finds that the majority are already scale-invariant by construction, and the token budgets plus the ledger retention are genuinely isolated cases, then T collapses into two ordinary defects and should be struck from the ladder rather than carried."
  - created_at: "2026-07-30T17:22:28.230Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Pass summary 2026-07-30 (evening) — bounded-output conformance, usage-ledger integrity, and intake organisation\n\nTheme: point the context-budget bundle that merged at 14:33Z at its own declarations, on this tracker rather than on a fixture. Identified by date and theme per the pass-identifier decision; no sequential number introduced.\n\nTracker state at pass end: 2,220 nodes, 10,041 edges, 320 active, 0 isolated, 0 degree-one-or-less, one component, zero orphans, ordering critical path 9 (up from 6), frontier 87.7 percent unchanged, eight known legacy ordering cycles unchanged. Validate warning classes went from six to four during the pass: the missing-estimate class and the orphaned-linked-path class are now zero.\n\nVerified shipped, recorded as evidence on the owners rather than closed by me. Bounded graph impact, 2,160 bytes default against 311,343 with the escape hatch, 144-fold. Verdict-first health check-only, 1,503 against 172,990, 115-fold. Intent projections now apply on list, get and search, all three inside their declared ceilings, replacing measured overruns of 43.8, 19.7 and 2.45 times. Unknown intents now produce one structured envelope with one exit code instead of two contradictory validators. The universal row contract reaches eleven of nineteen read surfaces. Release health: one cut today, npm latest 2026.7.30, npx and bunx both run the published artifact under bun 1.3.11, main CI green on six consecutive completed runs, and a nightly dispatched at the current head is green on all legs after a scheduled red on an older commit.\n\nFiled, all dedupe-checked, all reaching every outcome milestone through typed paths at distance one to three.\n\nBudget infeasibility, P1, under the output-budget contract. Three of five intents declare a ceiling below the smallest projection their command can emit, so the result is omitted on every default invocation, while the receipt reports being within budget in the same envelope. The same five intents all fit on a two-item workspace, which is what makes it a calibration-against-a-toy-corpus finding rather than a bug in enforcement.\n\nPer-page envelope amortisation, P1, under the same parent. Walking 315 items through the triage intent costs 13.8 times the unprojected single call, because eight chain-invariant metadata blocks are re-emitted on a two-row page: 3,125 bytes per row against 228. Blocks the infeasibility work, because calibrating a ceiling before the envelope is fixed means calibrating twice.\n\nUsage-ledger cost and scaling, P1, under the scale-out initiative. Every next and context read appends a serve entry of 10 to 16 kB carrying one row per open item on the context surface, then takes a workspace-global lock and rewrites the whole ledger, because the compaction trigger is a byte threshold that a count-based retention can never satisfy. Twelve concurrent reads take 5,145 milliseconds against 1,013 serial, with no loss and no corruption, so it is a throughput ceiling today and a hard scale wall at the stated million-item target.\n\nPhantom serve inclusions, P2, under the relevance engine. A response whose result was omitted still records 35 items as served and included, against 21 on the served control, so the affinity signal trains on context no agent received and the omitted case is indistinguishable from the delivered one.\n\nGovernance close-reason parity, P1, under governance and data quality. pm close refuses a reasonless close while pm update --status closed accepts one, synthesises the reason, and writes it into the immutable record with no marker distinguishing it from an author-supplied value. Two live items and three history streams already carry the synthesised literal. This is a re-escape of a class closed once on the create path.\n\nOrganisation. The five external intake items filed with the bundle were parented directly on this roadmap milestone, which the convention recorded here forbids; they are re-parented to their capability epics, given estimates, risk and confidence, and wired to the outcome milestones their work serves. That cleared the missing-estimate warning class entirely. Two contract specs shipped this afternoon were linked to the items they prove, with runnable commands, clearing the orphaned-path class. The daily release-health reminder, which fired on 2026-07-27 and was never re-armed, is re-armed for 2026-07-31, and the three-day silent lapse is recorded as evidence on the re-arming feature.\n\nPlanning extended, never restarted: horizon T on this milestone, one catalog entry by slug on the relevance engine with its owner split recorded on the portfolio registry, one new simplification axis counting the ways to bound a read, and ruling G on the grammar ADR."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T06:11:11.805Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Horizon U — the record is immutable at the entry and derived-from-mutable at the projection, so improving the record changes what the project says it shipped (2026-07-31)\n\nWhat was measured today. Of 1,904 terminal items, 1,488 carry neither closed_at nor completed_at — 78.2 percent. The changelog generator resolves an item's release section through the precedence chain completed_at, then closed_at, then updated_at. For those 1,488 items the release attribution is therefore a function of a mutable field, and any write to the item — a typed edge, a resolution backfill, a corrected dependency kind, a note recording why the work mattered — moves it out of the release section it shipped in and into the current one.\n\nThe consequence, which is the horizon. Enriching the project's history degrades the project's published statement about its history. The two things the roadmap most wants — a graph rich enough that the timeline can be reconstructed by traversal, and a changelog that truthfully says what shipped when — are in direct opposition under the current derivation. The project has already sensed this and responded procedurally: a maintained convention says passes are create-only on closed items. That convention is why the terminal ordering spine is still 87.7 percent frontier after a month of graph waves. A discipline is holding the line where a structural property should be.\n\nWhy this is not the same as provable history. The hash chain proves that no history entry was altered after it was written, and the portable attestation work will let a third party check that claim without trusting us. Both are statements about entries. Neither is a statement about projections. The changelog, the release notes, the graph profile, the flow metrics, the milestone ladder progress and every dashboard slice are derived artifacts: each is a function of the corpus evaluated at a moment, published as a statement about the past, and none of them records which corpus state produced it or what class of future write is permitted to change it. An immutable entry log with mutable public projections is not immutable where the reader looks.\n\nThe general shape. A derived artifact makes a claim of one of two kinds. Either it is a snapshot claim — this is what the workspace looked like when asked — in which case it must carry the corpus state that produced it and be reproducible from that state. Or it is a historical claim — this is what happened — in which case it must be invariant under every write that does not change what happened. The changelog is a historical claim computed as a snapshot claim, which is precisely why it is fragile. Today nothing in the product declares which kind any artifact is, so the distinction is made per-artifact, in an author's head, at generation time.\n\nWhat the horizon asks for, in order. First, a census: every artifact the project derives and publishes, labelled snapshot or historical. Second, for each historical artifact, the input fields it is permitted to depend on must be immutable-by-construction fields only; where a mutable field is currently load-bearing, that is a defect with a named remediation rather than a caveat. Third, for each snapshot artifact, the corpus state that produced it is recorded with it, so regeneration is verifiable rather than assumed. Fourth, a gate that fails when a historical artifact's derivation reads a mutable field — the same shape as the contract tests already used elsewhere, and cheap once the census exists.\n\nWhy this is a horizon rather than three defects. Three defects already exist for three instances: release attribution derived from a mutable timestamp, changelog rows ordered by a mutable timestamp, and the closed_at backfill that would remove the dependency for the one artifact anyone has noticed. Fixing all three leaves the property undeclared, so the fourth derived artifact — the flow-metric series, the render-ready export, the fleet attribution rollup, the milestone forecast — will be authored the same way, and nothing will object. Every one of those is planned. The census is cheap now, when there are roughly six derived artifacts, and expensive later.\n\nRelation to horizons already recorded. Horizon G asks for determinism as a global property: the same inputs produce the same output. This asks the prior question of which inputs a public claim is entitled to read. Horizon T asks that declared bounds be functions of the corpus they are enforced against; this asks that published claims about the past be functions only of things the past cannot change. Horizon S asks for generative closure so that no agent-facing surface can be hand-authored; the same argument applies to derived artifacts, and the census is the precondition for making it. Horizon R asks who could verify our guarantees from outside; a snapshot artifact that does not record its corpus state cannot be verified from outside even in principle.\n\nThe cheap first step, available now. The census is a list. The rule that a historical artifact may not read a mutable field is one sentence. Applying it to the one artifact that is already load-bearing — the changelog — is an existing, unblocked, priority-one item whose declared blocker closed on 2026-07-10 and which has been executable for twenty-one days. Executing it removes the discipline that is currently suppressing the entire terminal-graph programme, which is the largest single unlock available in the backlog.\n\nFalsifier. If the census finds that the changelog is the only derived artifact making a historical claim, and every other published derivation is honestly a snapshot of the present, then U is not a horizon: it is one defect with a known fix, and the ladder should not carry it."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T06:17:56.344Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Pass summary 2026-07-31 — derived-artifact provenance, the closed_at keystone, and one bounding vocabulary\n\nTheme: ask what is blocking the graph programme the roadmap has been requesting on every pass, then measure the blocker instead of restating the request. Identified by date and theme per the pass-identifier decision; no sequential number introduced.\n\nTracker state at pass end: 2,223 items, 319 open, 1,891 closed, 13 canceled, 42,120 history entries, zero drift across 2,223 streams, health verdict ok with zero warnings. Graph: 2,226 nodes, 10,107 edges, one component, zero orphans, zero isolated active nodes, zero active nodes of degree one or less, critical path 9, frontier 1,838 of 2,096 ordered nodes, eight known legacy ordering cycles unchanged, eleven informational audit findings unchanged.\n\nTwo measurements that reframe the graph programme.\n\nReachability is not the deficiency. Computed directly from stored dependency arrays, bypassing the active-node scoping the audit applies: every one of 2,226 nodes reaches an outcome milestone through a typed non-related path within five hops, terminal items included. Nine nodes carry exactly one edge kind, 1,850 carry three or more, and the maximum is seven. Parent depth maxes at six, which remains a fact about this corpus and must never be manufactured. The graph is reachability-saturated.\n\nOrdering is the deficiency, and it has a named keystone. 1,839 of 2,096 ordered nodes have no predecessor. The reason is not that ordering evidence is missing — extraction was declared exhausted in an earlier pass, correctly, as an authoring gap rather than an extraction gap. The reason is that authoring it is unaffordable: 1,488 of 1,904 terminal items carry neither closed_at nor completed_at, so the changelog generator resolves their release section through the updated_at fallback, and any write to them moves them out of the release they shipped in. The create-only-on-closed-items convention exists to prevent that, and it is precisely what has suppressed the terminal graph programme for a month. The closed_at backfill that removes the dependency has been executable since its declared blocker closed on 2026-07-10; the stale blocker edge is retired, priority raised to P1, and eight downstream programmes are now recorded as blocked by it in the graph rather than in prose: terminal-history backfill, resolution evidence backfill, duplicate issue-code rename, legacy edge hygiene, history-derived edge reconstruction, the ordering-evidence tranche, the historical program spine, and linked-file ownership reconciliation.\n\nFiled, all dedupe-checked against the full all-status corpus, all reaching every outcome milestone through typed paths.\n\nOne bounding vocabulary for every read, P1, under the simplification epic. Eighteen output-bounding flag spellings across nineteen read commands, no two commands sharing a set, four surfaces with none at all, widest pairwise availability disagreement ten, and the token-budget flag present on exactly the three surfaces that do not enforce declared ceilings while absent from the three that do. Recorded as blocking all eleven noun-consolidation slices, because two independently recorded axes concluded that folding nouns is a null move while an agent must still learn a per-command matrix to ask for less output.\n\nExtension-typed items are outside the merge fence, P1, under concurrency and multi-agent safety. Reproduced in a clean sandbox: install a domain pack, create an item of its type, and the item is written to a folder with no merge attribute while merge install reports the fence unchanged. Five such folders exist in this repository. The fence became project-schema-scoped deliberately, to stop local validation recommending a change CI reverts, and that closed decision carried the clause that it must not weaken custom item merge protection. It did. A tracker-wide toon pattern ahead of the per-folder rules restores coverage without letting extension state into the committed fence, so the determinism property is preserved.\n\nFour read surfaces publish an extraction contract that cannot return anything, P2, under CLI surface and ergonomics. The universal jq selector is a pure function of row_keys; deps, stats, aggregate and get publish it with row_keys empty. Running the documented one-liner against an item with eighteen dependency edges returns zero rows and exits zero. A missing contract fails loudly and is special-cased once; a false one fails silently and is indistinguishable from an empty result.\n\nFour edge-counting conventions under one field name, P2, under graph maturity. analyze reports 10,107 and centrality 7,351 for the same graph at the same cache fingerprint, because one emits directed stored edges and the other halves a degree sum; two further conventions exist in the fixture builder and the deps projection. edge_count is the before-and-after measure for every graph wave, and it currently has four meanings and no declared basis.\n\nPlanning extended, never restarted. Horizon U on this milestone: the record is immutable at the entry and derived-from-mutable at the projection, so improving the record changes what the project says it shipped; the horizon asks for a census of derived artifacts labelled snapshot or historical, and a rule that a historical claim may read only immutable inputs. One simplification axis on the epic: count the surfaces on which the product describes itself, then execute each self-description and count how many are true — seven of eleven published row contracts, with the false four costing more than no contract at all. One catalog entry by slug on the relevance engine, co-mutation-cohort-inference, with its owner split recorded on the portfolio registry: infer temporal association from cohorts of items mutated together in one actor session, which is the only ordering evidence available on a corpus whose ordering edges were never authored, is derivable from immutable history without writing to any item, and folds to bounded cost over decades. Ruling H on the grammar ADR: bounding dimensions are inherited by every read noun rather than chosen per command, inapplicable is reported rather than rejected as unknown, precedence is declared, the bounding set lands before any noun is moved, and every published self-description is gated as executable.\n\nRelease verified live. The scheduled cut ran once today and published 2026.7.31; npm latest, npx direct, npx with an explicit package, and bunx under bun 1.3.11 all resolve and execute the published artifact. The declared cron is 02:35 UTC and the run was created at 05:50:49Z, consistent with the stable three-hour drift already owned; three of the last eleven completed scheduled runs failed, consistent with the lifetime rate already owned. Evidence recorded on both owners rather than closed by this pass."
  - created_at: "2026-07-31T14:27:38.459Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Roadmap horizon V, recorded 2026-07-31 (afternoon). Method: ask whether the lifecycle preserves the facts the analytics depend on. Every prior horizon compared a declaration against a behaviour, or one surface against another. This one compares the immutable record against the current-state projection derived from it, and finds the projection lossy in exactly the dimension the roadmap most depends on.\n\nThe load-bearing finding is pm-xm0id4. clearClosedBlockerSignals removes every blocked_by dependency row when an item closes, unconditionally, without regard to whether the blocker is still open. Corpus signature: of 1,900 closed items exactly ONE retains a recorded predecessor, while the reverse spelling blocks, untouched by that code path, survives on 189. Reproduced in a sandbox with three still-open blockers, all three deleted at close, one named in the single warning. This reframes pm-gxs55a: the flat ordering graph - frontier 1,852 of 2,112 ordered nodes today - is not an authoring deficit, it is a deletion. It also blocks pm-ye46ns, whose recovery tranche writes recovered ordering into the exact field the lifecycle clears.\n\nThe second finding is pm-aka8m7, reached from the same question one layer down. reanchorHistoryEntries overwrites each entry's before_hash and after_hash in place and retains the prior pair nowhere; the merge driver documents re-anchoring as true for every union merge. So after any concurrent-branch merge the chain still verifies while no verifier can confirm an entry was written against the state its author observed. pm-w7ccmv promises immutably recorded AND independently verifiable; the first clause survives a merge and the second does not.\n\nTogether these say something about the shape of the remaining work that the epic partition does not currently express: the immutable stream is a strict superset of item state, and three separate roadmap threads are limited by reading the smaller one. Ordering analytics read state and lose closed-item sequence. Provenance analysis reads state-adjacent aggregates and never reads the corpus at all - pm-1wiugq: the analyzer that detects an inert identity signal is called only by its own unit test, while model coverage sits at 0 of 2,814. Trajectory and episode work reads the stream correctly and is gated on an identity that the stream records inconsistently - pm-1zhfls: one session, two surfaces, effort present on CLI-written entries and absent on MCP-written ones, permanently, because the MCP server is spawned without the signal and snapshots its environment at startup. The catalog entry recorded today on pm-4k6b, state-erased-signal-recovery, is the general form of the remedy: compute signals over edge-validity intervals folded from the stream rather than over presence in state.\n\nSurface direction, measured rather than asserted. 87 top-level spellings and 71 contract summaries, against roughly 60 when pm-n7rr opened 26 days ago - about one new spelling per day, a 45 percent larger surface, accumulated while the grammar ADR sat proposed. The fence behaves as written and does not resist: a new command trips a missing-baseline violation once, and the documented remedy regenerates the baseline, so required_commands is a 67-entry record of everything ever accepted and the gate passed green today across 91 surfaces. Payload grew too: context --limit 10 at 9,218 bytes against 7.6 KB on 07-24, list-open --limit 20 at 4,734 against 3.6 KB, contracts --flags-only at 124,288 against 117,070 on 07-26. Recommendation recorded on pm-pbyu: land the alias substrate before the decision rather than after, since a retired-spelling table is decision-independent, and denominate the fence in spellings rather than bytes.\n\nOrganization change: pm-klxax7 was filed as a programme container for the agent-environment work, which existed as five items parented across four capability epics with no owner for the seams between them. Constituents keep their capability parents and carry typed edges to the programme.\n\nLive release state verified rather than recalled: the scheduled run at 05:50Z succeeded, v2026.7.31 is latest, npm latest resolves 2026.7.31, and both npx and bunx resolve it. One release per UTC day is working. pm-uenmu9 records the open question that a second same-day release is architecturally impossible rather than merely gated, because resolveVersion pins the target to the UTC date key.\n\nWhat the next horizon should NOT redo. The ordering measurement is settled - the cause is deletion, filed, and its consequence for pm-ye46ns is recorded; do not re-measure frontier counts to rediscover it. Merge safety is thoroughly covered by the existing fence, receipts, reconciliation and CI enforcement; the only gap is anchor retention, now filed. The scale and reproducibility substrate is shipped (pm-rbcvt2, pm-mi2x, pm-vv2lti) - the gap there is a contract over it, not more primitives."
learnings[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-31T06:28:25.423Z","harness:claude-code","A convention adopted to protect an artifact can become the largest blocker in the backlog without anyone deciding it should be. The create-only-on-closed-items rule exists because 78 percent of terminal items resolve their changelog section from a mutable field. It is correct, it is cheap to follow, and it has silently suppressed the entire terminal graph programme for a month. When a discipline is standing in for a structural property, measure what the discipline is costing before adding another pass that works around it — the workaround is usually more expensive than the fix, and the fix here had been unblocked for 21 days."
tests[3]{command,scope,timeout_seconds}:
  pm health --check-only --summary --json,project,240
  pm validate --check-resolution --check-history-drift --json,project,240
  pnpm build,project,240
test_runs[1]{run_id,kind,status,started_at,finished_at,recorded_at,passed,failed,skipped}:
  test-local-mr9vjphg-6xwz4t,test,passed,"2026-07-06T23:51:30.213Z","2026-07-06T23:51:56.356Z","2026-07-06T23:51:56.356Z",3,0,0
body: "## Vision\npm is a universal, agent-optimized, git-native project management CLI that ships PM primitives via an SDK so any project can fully customize and optimize it. Guiding principle: **project management = context management** — an agent must be able to rebuild full working context from the pm CLI alone.\n\nThis milestone groups the post-v0.1 capability epics and their open backlog (surfaced by the 2026-05-31 ecosystem review pm-w7f2; last reconciled by review #4 pm-osea on 2026-07-04). PRD post-v0.1 roadmap items (advanced search tuning, adapter optimization, sandbox API surface) live under the relevant capability epic as concrete, dedup-checked work.\n\n## Living capability epics (index)\nEach is a permanently-open living backlog; new work in that area lands there. Scope In/Out boundaries are stated in each epic charter so no two overlap.\n\n- pm-mpbb — CLI command surface & agent ergonomics\n- pm-o2kc — Storage, history & data durability\n- pm-ydp6 — Search & semantic retrieval\n- pm-ugqx — Extension & package platform maturity (incl. SDK, plugins)\n- pm-33cw — Governance, validation & data quality\n- pm-dj98 — Concurrency & multi-agent safety\n- pm-5oj5 — Observability, telemetry & health\n- pm-8jdc — Schema, config & universal customization (incl. profiles)\n- pm-5k4v — MCP server & contract platform\n- pm-92if — CodeFactor / source-code quality (A→A+)\n- pm-u9d0 — Docs, onboarding, release & CI\n- pm-m2u1 — Architecture Decision Records (living decision log)\n\n## Convention\nCapability epics stay OPEN even with zero open children (living backlogs). Review passes are CREATE-ONLY on closed items (changelog buckets by updated_at). Every pm validate/health warning maps to exactly one open remediation item.\n\n## Update 2026-07-05 — strategic initiatives\nTwo cross-cutting initiative epics created (parented under their capability epics, indexed here for discoverability):\n- **pm-n7rr** (under pm-mpbb) — CLI surface simplification: noun-verb grammar, ~15 top-level nouns, zero feature loss, alias-preserving deprecation. ADR pm-pbyu.\n- **pm-usfg** (under pm-ugqx) — SDK-first architecture: the pm SDK becomes the single public API delivering all universal PM primitives; CLI/MCP are presentation layers. ADR pm-muhw.\nThese deliver this milestone's stated objective directly: a universal, fully-customizable PM tool whose baseline primitives ship via the pm SDK.\n\n## Update 2026-07-06 (review #13) — index completion + long-term horizons\nCapability-epic index addition (created by review #12, indexed here now):\n- pm-qwoy — Security & code scanning (scanner-import batches + per-finding issues)\n\nThird strategic initiative:\n- **pm-9rxu** (under pm-o2kc) — Scale-out: sub-second, bounded-token context at 100k-1M items with thousands of concurrent agents. ADR pm-bl8x. Children: pm-mi2x benchmark harness (do first), pm-801d persistent read index, pm-dfg0 cursor pagination, pm-0qg5 directory fan-out, pm-lvyd claim throughput, pm-o79o history/git growth policy, pm-g2zz agent story.\n\nComposability contract set (under pm-mpbb, delivers the stated grep/jq/xargs product principle): pm-m9xv story, pm-646c NDJSON output mode, pm-psy1 scripting contract.\n\nHorizon ordering of the initiatives (long-term plan):\n1. Near: pm-n7rr CLI simplification + pm-usfg SDK-first (mutually reinforcing; ADRs pm-pbyu/pm-muhw first, then pm-a22j/pm-lodl baselines).\n2. Mid: composability contract set + pm-qwoy zero-open-findings + governance/docs debt burn-down.\n3. Long: pm-9rxu scale-out (ADR pm-bl8x may be accepted earlier; implementation follows the SDK-first primitive layer so index/pagination land in the SDK, not as CLI forks).\n\n## Update 2026-07-06 (review #15) — horizon 4 + coverage completions\n\nBacklog coverage completions (review #15):\n- pm-xkgq (under pm-n7rr) — fold aggregate into the query nouns (last uncovered query-family command).\n- pm-npr3 (under pm-n7rr) — map init/config/schema/profile in the grammar table (last uncovered command family).\n- pm-x6jf (under pm-usfg) — promote package/extension lifecycle primitives (last uncovered SDK domain).\n- pm-rml5 (under pm-ugqx) — EXTEND the existing pm-github package (import provenance adoption, evidence-comment sync, metadata seeding): automates the per-review-pass GH-issue mirroring loop.\n\nHorizon 4 (post scale-out):\n- pm-agou (under pm-m2u1) — ADR: v1.0 GA stability contract (grammar freeze, SDK semver, format_version policy, scale targets). Draft after pm-pbyu and pm-bl8x are accepted.\n- pm-is1a (under pm-o2kc) — multi-workspace federation exploration story (cross-workspace context/deps/aggregation); spike + ADR only, parked at P4 until single-workspace primitives land.\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 (review #16) — long-horizon gap items\n\nFull all-status review (session pm-su60): GH-issue coverage 26/26, all validate warnings mapped (pm-e9zh, pm-pivf), pm-n7rr grammar partition and pm-usfg promotion partition re-verified complete. Four dedupe-checked long-horizon gaps filed:\n\n- pm-g5sx (under pm-o2kc) — tracker merge semantics: what \"mergeable\" (ADR pm-i7i4/pm-bl8x constraint) means per artifact class + post-merge reconciliation; prerequisite for pm-is1a federation. Horizon: long.\n- pm-e200 (under pm-dj98) — mutation event stream primitive (pm events NDJSON + SDK subscription, cursor-resumable, no daemon); deps pm-646c/pm-801d; pm-slack package is the first consumer. Horizon: mid-long.\n- pm-xc68 (under pm-33cw) — principal-based item-level policy exploration story (spike + ADR only, P4, dep pm-agou); layered-enforcement stance, feeds the pm-8ngt companies-on-pm-SDK vision. Horizon: 4+.\n- pm-pee5 (under pm-ugqx) — EXTEND pm-brief with flow metrics (cycle/lead time, throughput, aging WIP) from history streams, on SDK read primitives. Horizon: mid.\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 — all-status backlog review + long-horizon planning pass\nFull review across all 1657 items (open/closed/canceled) and the live command surface. Verified: GH-issue coverage 26/26; every pm validate warning maps to an open item (pm-e9zh close-reason evidence, pm-pivf GH-209 duplicate code); pm-n7rr grammar partition re-verified against the live 65-command surface (note on pm-n7rr); pm-usfg promotion partition unchanged and complete. Shipped-and-closed confirmation: restore-to-any-version (pm-9lc), history file recovery (pm-g6qd), per-stream hash-chain --verify, workspace-wide drift scan.\n\nThree dedupe-checked gaps filed:\n- **pm-klo8** (under pm-o2kc) — workspace-scoped audit history stream: schema/config/profile/init mutations currently leave NO append-only record (schema.ts/config.ts have zero history integration); closes the last hole in the every-mutation-leaves-immutable-proof invariant. Horizon: mid.\n- **pm-9yfo** (under pm-o2kc) — history completeness gate: contract-enumerated CI proof that every mutating CLI/SDK action appends history; phase 1 (item scope) can start now. Horizon: near-mid.\n- **pm-plwm** (under pm-9rxu) — index-backed --where query predicates so complex boolean/comparator selections stay O(result) at 100k-1M items instead of full-dump-through-jq; deps pm-801d/pm-dfg0, grammar placement per ADR pm-pbyu. Horizon: long.\n\nGrammar brainstorm additions recorded in place: shared-verb-vocabulary principle + measurable token-budget acceptance target proposed on ADR pm-pbyu; partition census note on pm-n7rr.\n\nHorizon map (unchanged ordering, new items placed): 1 near — pm-n7rr + pm-usfg (+ pm-9yfo phase 1); 2 mid — composability set, pm-qwoy findings burn-down, pm-klo8, pm-pee5, pm-e200; 3 long — pm-9rxu implementation (incl. pm-plwm), pm-g5sx merge semantics; 4+ — pm-agou GA contract, pm-is1a federation, pm-xc68 policy roles, pm-8ngt/pm-xtrd beyond-PM exemplars.\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 — measurement-and-memory planning pass\nSecond same-day all-status review; grammar/promotion partitions and validate-warning mapping re-confirmed unchanged (pm-e9zh, pm-pivf still the only two warnings, both mapped). Theme of this pass: the backlog *builds* the context engine and token-efficient surface but did not *measure or preserve* them over time. Three dedupe-checked items filed:\n\n- **pm-atfm** (under pm-9rxu) — context relevance evaluation harness: extends the shipped search-eval pattern (pm-u8n5) to pm context/next assembly; graded golden-workspace judgments, phase-1 baseline of the structural assembler BEFORE pm-4k6b lands, then per-signal attribution. Horizon: near-mid (phase 1 now).\n- **pm-cu1i** (under pm-n7rr) — token-cost regression gate: pm-a22j's one-time baseline becomes a permanent CI budget ratchet over a representative invocation corpus (eslint-suppressions discipline). Horizon: near, lands with the consolidation it protects.\n- **pm-5qmm** (under pm-9rxu) — workspace memory tiers: derived rollup summaries of closed-item epochs so decades-old work contributes bounded-token context; complements pm-o79o (disk cost) on the aging story; derived-only, never mutates tracked items. Horizon: long, after pm-801d/pm-4k6b; spike first.\n\nMeasurement triad now mirrors the initiative triad: pm-a22j+pm-cu1i measure token cost (pm-n7rr), pm-atfm measures context quality (pm-4k6b/pm-9rxu), pm-mi2x measures latency/memory (pm-9rxu).\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 — spectrum-ends and schema-evolution planning pass\nThird same-day all-status review; grammar partition (pm-n7rr), SDK promotion partition (pm-usfg), GH-issue coverage, and validate-warning mapping (pm-e9zh, pm-pivf) all re-confirmed unchanged. Theme of this pass: the backlog optimized the large end of the scale spectrum and the integrity of item history, but left the small end, schema aging, and author authenticity unanchored. Three dedupe-checked items filed:\n\n- **pm-dijg** (under pm-8jdc) — schema evolution migrations: rename/retire of custom types/fields/statuses currently strands existing items with stale values; lossless, history-recorded, O(affected) migration primitives (SDK-first, dry-run plan, resumable). Complements pm-z5nb (schema CRUD) and preserves the immutable-proof invariant through migrations. Horizon: long (after pm-3mna/pm-801d; design can start with pm-z5nb).\n- **pm-u14c** (under pm-o2kc, P4) — history author authenticity exploration story: hash chain = tamper-evidence, pm-3z0k = portable proof, this = verifiable authorship (PM_AUTHOR is self-asserted today), pm-xc68 = authorization. Spike + ADR only. Horizon: 4+, feeds the pm-8ngt companies-on-pm vision.\n- **pm-z436** (under pm-mpbb) — small-end spectrum story: quick-capture scratch profile, proportional token cost at 3-item scale, e2e budget assertion via the pm-cu1i corpus. Completes the spectrum pair with pm-g2zz (million-item end). Horizon: near-mid, lands with the lean-output family.\n\nScope notes recorded in place (no new items): workspace-level --at projection phase on pm-hib1; estimate-vs-actual calibration view on pm-pee5.\n\nHorizon map (placement of new items, ordering unchanged): 1 near — pm-z436 alongside the lean-output/token-budget work; 3 long — pm-dijg with the schema/customization completion; 4+ — pm-u14c with pm-xc68/pm-is1a/pm-agou.\n\n## Update 2026-07-07 — shipped-work reconciliation + adaptive-loop planning pass\nFourth same-day all-status review, focused (per the prior pass's note) on reconciling shipped work. Verified live: 1676 items, 0 in_progress, 0 blocked; history drift 0/1676; format_version current. GH-issue coverage re-walked against the live gh issue list: 24/25 mapped; the one gap (GH-476, filed after the last coverage pass) is now covered. All five validate warning classes map to open items (files -> pm-j8z6, close_reason/resolution evidence -> pm-e9zh, duplicate issue code -> pm-pivf).\n\nShipped-work reconciliation: PR #477 (f7e2ac21) closed its own lineage cleanly — pm-rjqr (query/read SDK primitives) and pm-98cz (item-lifecycle SDK primitives) are the first two pm-usfg promotion domains DONE, plus pm-qfte (GH-458 --assignee alias), pm-395t (structured create payloads), pm-25d0 (SDK package coordinates). Progress comment recorded on pm-usfg; 7 promotion domains + capstone remain.\n\nThree dedupe-checked items filed:\n- **pm-5h9g** (under pm-mpbb) — GH-476: pm context --max-items unknown_option needs an alias or targeted recovery hint; same class as shipped GH-458, generalizes under pm-gdi7. Horizon: near, lands with the lean-output/agent-UX family.\n- **pm-4ri6** (under pm-33cw) — create-time near-duplicate advisory: SDK findSimilarItems primitive + advisory on pm create/copy, sharing one similarity implementation with dedupe-audit; moves duplicate prevention from procedural (search-first discipline) to structural at thousand-agent concurrency. Advisory-by-default, index-bound, strict mode opt-in. Horizon: mid, after the search/index primitives it rides on.\n- **pm-uwfs** (under pm-4k6b) — context usage feedback signal: served-then-acted-on outcomes become a usage-affinity scoring input (retrieval-practice effect), implicit judgments for pm-atfm, and salience hints for pm-5qmm consolidation. Completes the pm-vvib cognitive map with its missing adaptive mechanism (comment recorded there). Horizon: long, phase 2+ of the context engine after pm-3hps/pm-h3no.\n\nHorizon map (ordering unchanged, new items placed): 1 near — pm-5h9g; 2 mid — pm-4ri6; 3 long — pm-uwfs with the context-engine implementation slices.\n\n## Update 2026-07-25 — outcome milestone ladder and long-horizon structure\n\nThis roadmap has carried horizons as prose since 2026-07-06. Prose horizons cannot be traversed, cannot be blocked, and cannot be closed. Six outcome Milestone items now materialise them as graph roots, so every active item can be asked the question \"which outcome does this serve\" and answered by traversal rather than by reading this body.\n\nThe ladder, in dependency order:\n\n- **pm-j82fd3 — agent-legible command grammar** (near, deadline 2026-10-31). Executed by pm-n7rr. Blocked by ADR pm-pbyu. Seventy-five top-level commands to a twelve-noun grammar with zero capability removed; the target noun table is recorded as a note on pm-n7rr.\n- **pm-9rgaal — SDK-complete core** (near-mid, deadline 2026-12-31). Executed by pm-usfg. The boundary ratchet becomes a hard gate and the remaining promotion slices close.\n- **pm-w7ccmv — provable history** (mid, deadline 2027-03-31). Executed by pm-o2kc. Closes the every-mutation-leaves-immutable-proof invariant: the completeness gate (pm-9yfo), the workspace audit stream (pm-klo8), portable attestation (pm-3z0k), and verifiable authorship (pm-u14c).\n- **pm-o87av6 — proven scale** (long, deadline 2027-09-30). Executed by pm-9rxu. Blocked by SDK-complete so the index and pagination land in the SDK rather than as CLI forks.\n- **pm-xp1xsw — v1.0 GA stability contract** (gate, deadline 2027-12-31). Blocked by grammar, SDK-complete, and provable-history; publishes only the scale numbers the scale milestone actually measured. Drafted by ADR pm-agou.\n- **pm-t4d7nz — universal domain platform** (far, deadline 2028-06-30). Blocked by SDK-complete and provable-history. The two shipped foreign-domain exemplars produced the gap ledger; this milestone closes it.\n\n### Detection capability filed the same pass\n\nThe pipeline is deep but its improvement was previously undirected. Five items now make detection a tracked capability rather than an emergent one, all reachable from the milestones above: pm-zclzll mutation-testing gate (coverage proves execution, not assertion), pm-rtn5h6 defect-escape ratchet (every escape lands a gate), pm-a8lnoh deterministic fault and concurrency simulation (the three known open races are its acceptance targets), pm-pae3wo cross-version compatibility matrix (a staggered agent fleet is the normal case), and pm-76dnfg continuous multi-branch merge conformance (merge safety shipped and closed; nothing continuously proves it still holds).\n\n### Agent ergonomics\n\npm-z9x1r2 removes the last piece of per-call boilerplate: the acting agent is resolved from its runtime harness, so PM_AUTHOR stops being a precondition for correct attribution and starts being an override. pm-5t33or promotes token budgets from one command to the contract layer, so no invocation can silently exhaust a context window.\n\n### Graph structure\n\npm-bzmeaa replaces the isolate-count floor with a reachability invariant: depth, eccentricity, shortest typed path to an outcome milestone, and edge-kind diversity along it. Two reconstruction waves were added under the plan pm-a8zm — pm-hnc9w7 for the changelog-safe legacy edge hygiene batch the audit has been requesting on every run, and pm-s5e04g for deriving evidence, incident, release, and package edges from immutable history rather than from similarity.\n\n### Convention unchanged\n\nCapability epics stay open as living backlogs. Outcome milestones are gates, not workstreams: they close when their acceptance criteria are met by the epics beneath them, and they never accumulate direct children of their own beyond the ladder recorded here.\n\n## Convention addition 2026-07-25 — pass identifiers (decision pm-zeifd2)\nReview, audit, organization, and planning passes are identified by ISO date plus a descriptive theme, never by sequential numbers. Numbered identifiers require global counter coordination that is unsafe under concurrent agents and branch merges, and carry no intrinsic context. Historical numbered references (for example review #13 or pass 91) remain unaltered as immutable record.\n## Update 2026-07-26 — ecosystem review: all-status walk, graph depth, agent ergonomics, release-pipeline verification (session pm-v4iypw)\n\nFull walk of all 13 capability epics, 4 strategic initiatives, and the outcome-milestone ladder against the stated product principles (session item pm-v4iypw). Tracker state at pass time: 2113 items, 308 open, 0 in_progress, 0 isolated active nodes, 0 degree-≤1 active nodes (graph audit); 2116 nodes / 9232 edges; edge-kind mix still related-heavy (related 4417 = 48%), with reclassification already tracked by pm-s5e04g (history-derived typing) and pm-4020c5 (lexicon canonicalization).\n\nVerified live (evidence comments recorded in place):\n- Daily auto-release healthy and same-day-idempotent: npm latest=2026.7.26; maybeSkipForSameDayRelease() + no-changes/tracker-only skips in run-release-pipeline.mjs; issues-path guards (same-UTC-day window, one-retry marker, tag-exists→rerun-not-duplicate); post-publish executor verification covers npm metadata, npx direct, npx --package, and bunx --bun (pm-7zs0). Open hardening stays with pm-lpqln4 and pm-2z9263.\n- Harness auto-attribution works end-to-end: this pass ran with no PM_AUTHOR/--author and recorded harness:opencode with detected provenance (pm-z9x1r2 shipped). Reasoning-level/session-role schema remains with pm-itsjf0; inert model capture with pm-0zcwz6.\n\nGaps filed (all dedupe-checked, horizon-placed):\n- **pm-a5w7vv** (under pm-ydp6, P4, spike+ADR) — natural-language intent compilation onto the structured query surface; the compiled query is the product, acceptance measured by pm-p1g43q. Horizon 4+, after the pm-agou grammar freeze stabilizes the compilation target.\n- **pm-0d7ord** (under pm-ugqx, P3) — render-ready workspace export + reference pm-dashboard package: bounded typed graph slices, pm-pee5 flow series, milestone-ladder progress, pm-gw6uyq fleet attribution as versioned JSON/NDJSON on the read index; blocked_by pm-801d. Horizon far.\n- **pm-hipfu9** (under pm-mpbb, P3) — recurring reminders that re-arm on fire; found because pm-7zs0 described a recurring check but carried zero reminders (never fireable). First daily reminder scheduled 2026-07-27T06:00Z as interim manual cadence. Horizon near-mid.\n\nConvention compliance: pass identified by date+theme per decision pm-zeifd2; no sequential numbering introduced anywhere in this pass."
